{"offset": 0, "next": 100, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "04d2ffda334877a42c714256c83f20b7b571e667", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07624", "CorpusId": 295594843}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/04d2ffda334877a42c714256c83f20b7b571e667", "title": "Beneath the Surface: What We Overlook When We Talk About Agent Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07624, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297250786", "name": "Xiaobo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2282562956", "name": "Zi-Mu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2330241161", "name": "Si-Yu Ye"}, {"authorId": "2265797420", "name": "Jia-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2526809778", "name": "Min Tang"}, {"authorId": "2505784112", "name": "Jun-Bo Bai"}, {"authorId": "2279412470", "name": "Yu-Ling Shi"}, {"authorId": "2512656473", "name": "Ruo-Bing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2518068418", "name": "Jing-Jing Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2356588864", "name": "Chao-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2286835859", "name": "K. Wu"}, {"authorId": "2339259770", "name": "Meng-Nan Qi"}, {"authorId": "2341532207", "name": "Kai Cai"}, {"authorId": "2374416817", "name": "Xiao-Dong Gu"}, {"authorId": "2525002153", "name": "Qing-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2266461235", "name": "Zi-Long Zheng"}], "abstract": "Agent harness evolution enables recursive self-improvement (RSI) without updating model weights. We examine whether higher benchmark scores yield reliable, efficient, and transferable improvement. We evaluate four methods across harnesses, general and coding tasks. Repeated evaluations, controlled agent expansion, component interventions, and admission replays reveal recurring patterns. All sixteen evolved agents gain previously uncovered tasks while losing previously covered ones. In one general-task case, reliability rises by 5.2 points despite losing ten previously covered tasks. Most reliability gains also increase deployment cost. Specific changes usually outperform generic reminders, but their benefits can change when combined. The highest-scoring agent does not yield the largest mean improvement in any of our four pools, and stricter admission can discard useful alternatives. Transfer selectively preserves gains: coverage usually falls on the general-task target across both harness types and paradigms, but rises on the coding target with the outer-layer-only harness. These findings motivate preservation-aware evaluation, tested decision rules, and explicit applicability conditions. As a constructive follow-up, EvoAudit turns these diagnostics into revisions of select, analyze, mutate, and gate, achieving the best performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "975e721203aaf1cfb1e47467e7856e1c93b23921", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07495", "CorpusId": 295593499}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/975e721203aaf1cfb1e47467e7856e1c93b23921", "title": "Learning Multi-Agent Orchestration:Higher-Order Interactions in Policy Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07495, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2364198578", "name": "Kai Liu"}, {"authorId": "2325728161", "name": "Guo-Jun Xiong"}], "abstract": "Learned orchestration has emerged as a promising paradigm for large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems, in which a central orchestrator selects agents and controls the information they receive throughout a task. Despite rapid empirical progress, existing methods train the orchestrator by crediting each decision with its own advantage, implicitly assuming that the other decisions in the episode stay fixed. This assumption fails in orchestration, where the contribution of each decision to the final outcome depends on the decisions preceding it, so the gain of a joint update is not the sum of its per-decision effects. To address this challenge, we introduce \\textbf{IAPO}, an interaction-aware policy optimization framework that explicitly accounts for interactions among decisions. Specifically, IAPO exactly decomposes the performance difference between two policies into the effects of individual decisions and their interactions, which we prove can account for half of the attainable gain, and then optimizes both from sampled trajectories while certifying that every update is an improvement. On long-input versions of GSM8K, MATH, DROP, MBPP and HumanEval, against fifteen published multi-agent and workflow-search systems on the same 7B executor, IAPO's deployed plans reach the strongest system's accuracy with fewer tokens, and removing the interaction term costs 7 to 17 points of accuracy on code."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3aceea8bec22ee7bd89fabc75b66c9fe6981edb2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.06768", "CorpusId": 295588863}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3aceea8bec22ee7bd89fabc75b66c9fe6981edb2", "title": "PyRUA-REAL: Robot Exploration and Adaptive Learning through RSI in the Real World", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06768, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2506666634", "name": "Qiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2190428643", "name": "Jian-Xin Bi"}, {"authorId": "2385327322", "name": "Shu-Long Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2383166153", "name": "Rui-Yang Si"}, {"authorId": "2311570567", "name": "Wen-Bo Huang"}, {"authorId": "2286953962", "name": "Kai-Qi Chen"}, {"authorId": "2445495190", "name": "Jia-Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2107939582", "name": "Li-Yun Li"}, {"authorId": "2504354514", "name": "Xuan-Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2404260527", "name": "Da-Quan Zhou"}], "abstract": "Coding agents powered by models such as GPT-6-Astra connect spatial reasoning with executable robot behavior, raising the question of how such agents can produce lasting improvements in robot capabilities from physical interaction. In this work, we study embodied recursive self-improvement (RSI) through real-world exploration, code refinement, and deployment using PyRUA-REAL, a framework in which coding agents retain interaction experience as persistent memory and reusable executable skills that inform subsequent exploration and improvement. Our empirical study places physical manipulation at the center of the improvement process, examining how agents collaborate, what kinds of execution feedback support effective code refinement, and how task specifications and previously acquired skills influence robustness and task transfer. We further investigate the efficiency and capability of the resulting programs when deployed without involving an online agent for decision-making. Across eight real-world tasks, PyRUA-REAL achieves an average success rate of 91.1%. In complementary evaluation on 42 tasks from the RoboDojo benchmark, it achieves an average success rate of 84.4%. This study provides empirical guidance for turning physical exploration into reusable robot skills that support continued improvement and robust, efficient deployment."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c8787c68818e163546244093dc32b663246d5610", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.06254", "CorpusId": 295589254}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c8787c68818e163546244093dc32b663246d5610", "title": "HarnessFlow: MCTS-Guided Harness Optimization for LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06254, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2455191447", "name": "Zhi-Cong Hong"}, {"authorId": "2452524953", "name": "Qian-Long Wang"}, {"authorId": "2228362755", "name": "Hong-Chen Wei"}, {"authorId": "2467017313", "name": "Ai-Dan Wu"}, {"authorId": "2338066707", "name": "Dao-Hong Ying"}, {"authorId": "2343658051", "name": "Ming-Ze Kong"}, {"authorId": "2382941490", "name": "Bei Liu"}, {"authorId": "2249532171", "name": "Chong Luo"}], "abstract": "The performance of large language model (LLM) agents depends on both the underlying model and the harness that governs interaction with tools and the environment. Yet harness design remains largely manual, and automating it requires navigating an open-ended search space through costly end-to-end evaluations. We introduce HarnessFlow, a framework for optimizing complete executable harnesses under a limited candidate-evaluation budget, with the model and task environment held fixed. HarnessFlow organizes candidates and their evaluation histories in a refinement tree, using a Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS)-based policy to balance exploitation and exploration. A coding agent refines selected harnesses using archived code, execution feedback, and a library of reusable harness mechanisms. With 15 refinement attempts per benchmark, HarnessFlow achieves the highest mean test scores among the compared methods on six benchmarks spanning agentic tasks, coding, and web interaction. The optimized harnesses achieve an average score of 69.48%, outperforming the initial harnesses and Meta-Harness by 10.89 and 3.95 percentage points, respectively, while incurring lower average per-task execution costs than hand-engineered baselines. These results suggest that structured harness search offers a promising route toward automating the design of capable and efficient LLM agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "329aa84f8489c74dcf06946c8e9a5b55c27a8c54", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04437", "CorpusId": 295506501}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/329aa84f8489c74dcf06946c8e9a5b55c27a8c54", "title": "WiSI: Wireless-in-the-Loop Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04437, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Engineering", "Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2238140933", "name": "Zong-Xi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2363514324", "name": "Hong-Yang Du"}], "abstract": "The growing autonomy of AI agents has enabled self-improvement, where agents iteratively refine their programs through execution feedback and performance evaluation. As these agents increasingly operate in physical environments and rely on wireless connectivity, communication conditions become an integral part of their improvement process. However, existing approaches largely assume ideal communication, overlooking how wireless conditions shape both the feedback that guides program modifications and the evaluation outcomes that determine which modifications survive. This challenge is further complicated by the coupling between agent behavior and wireless connectivity, as agents influence their own communication conditions through mobility and transmission decisions. To address this problem, we propose WiSI, a wireless-in-the-loop self-improvement framework that integrates spatiotemporal wireless channels, edge-assisted perception, and Large Language Model-driven program improvement. By incorporating wireless execution into the improvement loop, WiSI reveals how wireless execution experience shapes the generation and retention of program structures, and investigates the wireless conditions needed for robust improvement. Experiments show that agents improved under ideal links achieve only 40.0% task success under congestion, compared with 77.2% when improved under realistic wireless conditions. More importantly, wireless-aware feedback and selection jointly enable agents to discover and retain communication-efficient program structures that outperform parameter tuning and remain robust even with inaccurate run-time channel observations. We further find that moderate-load or mixed-network evaluation maintains strong performance across deployment conditions, while simplified models preserving link-rate statistics achieve comparable results without detailed spatial channel information."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8fc7172dc514e7004fd14bca5029e231d0a18ae8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05320", "CorpusId": 295506857}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8fc7172dc514e7004fd14bca5029e231d0a18ae8", "title": "PyRUA-DataFarm: LLM Agent as a Cost-Efficient Teleoperator for Real-Ready Robot Data", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05320, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362922379", "name": "Ke-Wei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2367736494", "name": "Jun Ma"}, {"authorId": "2526102471", "name": "Guo-Dong Lv"}, {"authorId": "2341662997", "name": "Duo-Min Wang"}, {"authorId": "2480056713", "name": "Fei-Xiang Ruan"}, {"authorId": "2383166153", "name": "Rui-Yang Si"}, {"authorId": "2385947083", "name": "Chuan-Rui Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2526363415", "name": "Yuxuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2526346210", "name": "Hao Chen"}, {"authorId": "2526372657", "name": "Yi-Chen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2440685250", "name": "Yintianrun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2269764462", "name": "Yu-Fan Deng"}, {"authorId": "41020000", "name": "En-Ze Xie"}, {"authorId": "2404260527", "name": "Da-Quan Zhou"}], "abstract": "Teleoperation remains the default way to collect robot demonstrations, but every episode costs robot and operator time, and every new task starts from scratch. We ask whether a coding agent can take over the teleoperator's job. In our DataFarm, the agent receives a data request and one calibration episode of the robot in the target scene and rebuilds the task in simulation: it writes the scene, an expert controller, and a success judge. The simulator supplies exact actions, and a video model conditioned on real images renders each rollout as photographic video, generating all camera views together consistently. The output is real-ready data: synchronized camera views labelled with the robot's actions, on which a policy can be trained directly and deployed on the real robot. Independent judges certify the expert and every rendered episode before admission. On five real-robot tasks, policies trained only on farm data match policies trained on as many teleoperated episodes (93 vs. 84 successes in 100 trials), and under an unseen tablecloth and coloured light they keep 87 successes where the teleoperation-trained policies fall to 46. Adapting a task is fully automatic and costs \\$21 of API usage on average, paid once per task, plus \\$5 for a render contract; every further episode costs \\$0.80 on average for simulation, rendering, and review, with no robot or operator. One hour of three-view data for a task costs \\$107 on average, these costs included, slightly below a quote of \\$117 for teleoperation in China and below \\$290 in labour alone in North America."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2319fdfe8347a7f8f5d52be898ba33012faf58b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05139", "CorpusId": 295508181}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2319fdfe8347a7f8f5d52be898ba33012faf58b9", "title": "Meta$^2$: Precomputing Reusable Knowledge for Online Harness Evolution in Long-Horizon Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05139, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2340603961", "name": "Lingzhi Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2526199633", "name": "Yi-Guang Cai"}, {"authorId": "1390516186", "name": "L"}, {"authorId": "2526386878", "name": "Si-Qi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2202174378", "name": "Fei-Jiang Han"}, {"authorId": "2509361763", "name": "Yongkyun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2307917108", "name": "Udari Madhushani Sehwag"}, {"authorId": "2398004564", "name": "Fu-Rong Huang"}], "abstract": "Harness optimization improves long-horizon agents by refining the prompts, tools, memory, and hooks around a frozen model. Offline methods can learn from completed trajectories, but deploy one shared harness that may overfit training tasks and cannot react to evidence revealed during a new run. Online evolution can adapt before, during, or after one run through an evolver, but may have to reason about every intervention from scratch or rely on verifier feedback that is usually unavailable at test time. We introduce Meta$^2$, which moves offline optimization one level up: rather than optimizing a shared harness, it learns reusable knowledge for harness evolution and applies it within each task online. Offline, Meta$^2$ learns a textual Meta-Skill $M$ by contrasting training rollouts of the same task agent under three configurations: without an evolver, with an evolver guided by the current version of $M$, and with an evolver given privileged verifier information available only in training. Contrasts in trajectories and outcomes are aggregated across tasks to refine $M$ into reusable guidance on when and how to evolve, when to leave the harness unchanged, and which interventions to avoid. Online, an evolver combines the frozen $M$ with emerging trajectory evidence to decide whether to KEEP the harness unchanged or EVOLVE it through a task-local edit. Each edit applies to the rest of the same run without resetting its environment or progress. Thus, evolution guidance transfers across tasks while harness edits remain task-local, and held-out verifier feedback is never available to the evolver. On two long-horizon benchmarks, Terminal-Bench 2.1 and DeepSWE 1.1, Meta$^2$ achieves the highest performance among evaluated methods with GPT-5.6-Terra, reaching 81.00% and 44.44%, respectively. Ablations support the value of contrastive evolution guidance and keeping online harness edits task-local."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "09ac7074cc803c86adcdfd3d80593cb399f332b4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03618", "CorpusId": 295503160}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/09ac7074cc803c86adcdfd3d80593cb399f332b4", "title": "Strategist: Evolving Reasoning with Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03618, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2308418220", "name": "Taiming Lu"}, {"authorId": "2326321062", "name": "Qi Xu"}, {"authorId": "2105837204", "name": "Zhi-Gang Hua"}, {"authorId": "2349957935", "name": "Zhuang Liu"}], "abstract": "A model is only half of an AI agent system. The other half is the inference strategy: the reasoning procedure that turns model calls into a response. Today that second half is written by hand, and the experience of building one strategy never carries to the next. We introduce Strategist, a meta-agent that evolves inference strategies across tasks. Each strategy is written as a blueprint,executable code that other strategies can reuse, and together the blueprints form a library. Given a task and a small development set, Strategist retrieves related blueprints, writes candidate strategies, tests them on real samples, and revises them from the results. Every candidate then enters the library, so later tasks build on earlier discoveries. On 30 benchmarks, Strategist beats the strongest baseline on 24 and matches its average accuracy (74.7% vs.74.0%) at 39% lower inference cost. Its strategies also transfer to unseen tasks and other models without further search. The part of an AI system that is not improved on its own now does."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6c6fe4a0fbf445aa4e4e12b909a300eec2c136f6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02797", "CorpusId": 295502389}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6c6fe4a0fbf445aa4e4e12b909a300eec2c136f6", "title": "SelfImprove-Bench: Evaluating Generalization in Self-Improving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02797, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2382941198", "name": "Bang-Rui Xu"}, {"authorId": "2526194103", "name": "Jie Yang"}, {"authorId": "2479245941", "name": "Si-Di Chen"}, {"authorId": "2403662638", "name": "Xuan-He Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2289911484", "name": "Jian-Tao Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2493634270", "name": "Qian-Yu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2499852383", "name": "Zi-Jie Wang"}, {"authorId": "2257429642", "name": "Fan Wu"}, {"authorId": "2325139162", "name": "Zhoufutu Wen"}], "abstract": "Recursively self-improving (RSI) systems are increasingly viewed as a promising means of accelerating progress toward artificial general intelligence. They aim to autonomously improve their capabilities by refining their working pipelines and recursively improving the strategies that guide subsequent refinements, with gains generalizing to unseen scenarios.However, existing evaluations lack reliable metrics to distinguish capability gains from scenario fitting, sustainable and extensible protocols for unseen tasks and difficulties, and fine-grained attribution of improvement to evolution traces. To this end, we propose SelfImprove-Bench,an RSI evaluation framework that organizes tasks into three primary capabilities (i.e., Exploring, Planning, Reasoning) as scenarios for guiding model evolution. We instantiated the tasks on games for controllability and reproducibility, and pairs them with scalable generation and orchestration driven by task templates and difficulty coefficients. Beyond task-level scores, SelfImprove-Bench organizes evaluation into generalization ladder levels G0 to G4 that measure how far gains transfer from training conditions. Experiments reveal that the evolutionary gains of existing RSI methods are inconsistent across generalization levels."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "388f73531930c3cc384f624d94bbf8bf495da0d4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04035", "CorpusId": 295507243}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/388f73531930c3cc384f624d94bbf8bf495da0d4", "title": "Recursa: Toward Recursively Self-Improving Foundation Model Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04035, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2253476816", "name": "Er-Kang Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2117033724", "name": "Yin Lin"}, {"authorId": "2330462706", "name": "Xiao-Rong Shi"}, {"authorId": "2300408961", "name": "Xuan-Rui Lin"}, {"authorId": "2237499232", "name": "Jing-Ren Zhou"}], "abstract": "Foundation models in changing environments can encounter unfamiliar domain knowledge that their original training did not cover. However, new domains rarely provide large amounts of expert-created training data or reliable supervisory signals for further training. We introduce Recursa, a foundation model system that adapts to new domains by learning from its own experience without human experts or a stronger teacher model. Recursa uses a knowledge repository to store what it learns: domain-specific skills and instructions on when to apply them, along with memories of past task experiences and reflections on what to improve. Recursa bootstraps the repository from domain materials and then refines it through recursive self-improvement. It uses parallel learners to execute tasks and synthesize local improvements, then validates and merges the improvements into the knowledge repository. On Harvey LAB's Firm Knowledge benchmark, Recursa raises the all-pass rate on held-out test tasks from 30.0% to 55.0% with DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash and from 35.0% to 46.0% with Qwen3.8-Flash."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b6f6aec5107383f06c3a6621f2db3aa9737536f0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03935", "CorpusId": 295507766}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b6f6aec5107383f06c3a6621f2db3aa9737536f0", "title": "Labtune: Treating Lab Organization and Information Sharing as Experimental Variables in LLM-Agent Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03935, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Physics", "Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2202983116", "name": "Goran Mitevski"}], "abstract": "De-siloing can create groupthink; silos can prevent knowledge sharing and reduce research efficiency. The same tension appears in research systems built with large language model (LLM) agents, yet their organization is usually fixed by design. I propose treating laboratory structure and information sharing as experimental variables. I introduce Labtune, an open framework for bounded research workflows with user-selected laboratory organization, communication policy, model configuration and budget. Users can choose among six editable designs, from multidisciplinary silos and transdisciplinary teams to co-leaders, hub-and-spoke structures, flat co-labs and a single scientist. Information sharing can independently vary from none to high detail, with an optional channel for conditional abstractions called wisdom. Sharing is enforced by the controller at phase barriers rather than through unrestricted agent chat, while delegation does not create additional budget or permissions. Development evidence is separated from protected confirmation, and an optional multi-lab layer allows laboratories with different structures to exchange filtered information. Labtune is an idea and system-description paper, not a benchmark result. Its contribution is to make laboratory organization and information sharing configurable and measurable under matched budgets. I do not claim that one design or level of sharing is best; the aim is to create a concrete experimental space in which those differences can be tested."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e27863b1a58125e17ba38efd3c31efbe66dc5807", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "conf/iclr/VuAJRG26", "ArXiv": "2611.01870", "CorpusId": 295408740}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e27863b1a58125e17ba38efd3c31efbe66dc5807", "title": "Automated Stateful Specialization for Adaptive Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01870, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2525216708", "name": "Myan Vu"}, {"authorId": "2525262818", "name": "Harrish Ayyanar"}, {"authorId": "2526030604", "name": "Jiang Pang"}, {"authorId": "2448063842", "name": "A. Reddy"}, {"authorId": "2525266013", "name": "Mayank Goel"}], "abstract": "Current automated agent design frameworks produce either static workflows that lack adaptability or per-query optimizers that prevent the accumulation of deep, agent-level task expertise. We propose a new direction that reconciles these paradigms: creating stateful teams of specialist agents that accumulate knowledge over time and can be reconfigured for novel tasks entirely without human intervention. To this end, we introduce ASpec, a framework that manages this full agent lifecycle by first autonomously discovering specialist archetypes via evolutionary search and then cultivating their expertise through experience, mirroring how human experts learn through practice and reflection. We further introduce a lightweight hierarchical control policy,\"retain-then-escalate,\"which governs when to leverage the established agent system versus when to adapt its structure. Through comprehensive experiments, we demonstrate that this approach leads to significant performance gains on expert-level scientific benchmarks like GPQA while matching the state-of-the-art on broader domain tasks, demonstrating a promising path toward agent systems that are simultaneously expert, adaptive, and efficient. We will release the code at https://github.com/myanvoos/ASpec."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ff1cf51ce122b98f55441e6cf408eb0e57ad3768", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01840", "CorpusId": 295502976}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ff1cf51ce122b98f55441e6cf408eb0e57ad3768", "title": "Self-Evolving Agentic Workflows via Semantic Decomposition of Complex Tasks", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01840, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2275537250", "name": "Zi-Chuan Fu"}, {"authorId": "2245589862", "name": "Jun-Jian Qi"}, {"authorId": "2277462592", "name": "Xian Wu"}, {"authorId": "2294381452", "name": "Wen-Lin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2328003718", "name": "Yi-Min Deng"}, {"authorId": "2238030670", "name": "Kai-Feng Guo"}, {"authorId": "2281902096", "name": "Xiang-Yu Zhao"}], "abstract": "Agentic workflows organize complex tasks into reusable procedures. However, invoking an LLM at every step incurs repeated inference costs and delays. We propose a framework for self-evolving agentic workflows through semantic task decomposition. Each subtask has explicit applicability conditions and a procedure that executes code or invokes a model as needed. An agent uses execution feedback to refine the decomposition and update these procedures. Experiments in the GLEE competition show the framework on bargaining, negotiation and persuasion. Most observed final-decision procedures are implemented in code. Code is available at https://github.com/Applied-Machine-Learning-Lab/NeurIPS2026_SeaFlow."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b3e80c23dbd8e490f79c10a84ed3fe6c00a67b45", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01415", "CorpusId": 295502584}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b3e80c23dbd8e490f79c10a84ed3fe6c00a67b45", "title": "Latent Harness: Compiling Agent Behaviors into Post-Block Activation Shifts", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01415, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2310570226", "name": "Ran Li"}, {"authorId": "2345012341", "name": "Shi-Min Di"}, {"authorId": "2374309246", "name": "Ze-Yu Ren"}, {"authorId": "2359688261", "name": "Ling Yue"}, {"authorId": "2275540444", "name": "Jia-Chuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2275866045", "name": "Lei Chen"}], "abstract": "Harness engineering and self-evolving agents improve language-model agents quickly, yet their gains live outside the model. The harness is re-sent and re-paid on every call, and the standard route to internalization is a weight update that is costly to iterate and fixed once merged. We propose compiling the harness instead. Latent Harness distills an external condition into a rank-8 activation shift. The shift is applied at the output of every decoder block of a frozen backbone, at 0.03-0.05% of parameters. The condition is either an explicit process-level behavior instruction or the behavioral contract of an agentic harness. At inference the compiled bundle carries no harness text and adds no tokens. It exposes three runtime controls for injection strength, injection phase, and shift-space mixing. Compiled behaviors recover the source-harness gain in 8 of 10 preregistered settings. Where the explicit source pays the heaviest per-call token tax, the compiled form exceeds it. The same bundles transfer zero-shot across tasks (5 of 7 pairs positive) and compose in shift space, while stacked text instructions interfere destructively. Used as a search space, they lift budget-matched harness search by 1.45 times. On three agentic benchmarks the operator aligns policy to the harness con- tract. Under matched data, capacity, and effective strength it outperforms same-capacity LoRA, which needs 11.1 times the parameters to reach the same band, and it regresses nothing where the base policy is already strong. Across 1B-to-8B models the method skeleton is invariant while gain locations move with the model-task pair, which delimits where compiled harnesses should be deployed."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b8fad5f0f454dbb263a925745aaabe78c364c3cc", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01377", "CorpusId": 295503479}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b8fad5f0f454dbb263a925745aaabe78c364c3cc", "title": "A Self-Exploring and Self-Evolving Paradigm for Adaptive Edge AI Development", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01377, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2134677112", "name": "Leming Shen"}, {"authorId": "2353439028", "name": "Qiang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2348879370", "name": "Xin-Yu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2348270288", "name": "Zi-Jing Ma"}, {"authorId": "2298902164", "name": "Yuan-Qing Zheng"}, {"authorId": "30601755", "name": "C. X. Lu"}], "abstract": "Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit powerful code generation abilities, fostering agentic systems to automate edge AI application development. However, existing agents typically operate via fixed agentic workflows and predefined design spaces, struggling to handle rapidly evolving demands of real-world scenarios (e.g., heterogeneous data modalities, diverse task pipelines). Unlike prior systems that treat LLMs mainly as code generators following rigid workflows, we fully exploit their reasoning capabilities to allow agents to determine their own workflows on the fly. To this end, we propose SE2, a self-exploring and self-evolving paradigm for adaptive edge AI development. Inspired by human learning processes, we distill development into four atomic operations (retrieve, reason, code, test), and empower agents to explore workflows by dynamically and adaptively selecting an appropriate next operation based on the current context. Extensive evaluations show that, with adaptive workflows, the synthesized programs achieve up to 26% higher accuracy and consume 28% lower resources than state-of-the-art baselines, while maintaining superior adaptability to unforeseen edge AI scenarios. This highlights the potential of reasoning-driven adaptive agentic workflow in edge AI development."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d93dfb7d9070ce7d9bcd18d5a433a128b359bbc8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.00643", "CorpusId": 295504218}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d93dfb7d9070ce7d9bcd18d5a433a128b359bbc8", "title": "Selecting LLM-Generated Hypotheses Raises Truth Beyond What an LLM Panel Expects: A Pre-registered Study on NAS-Bench-201 and HPOBench", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00643, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-31", "authors": [{"authorId": "2347371806", "name": "Shang-Lin Yang"}, {"authorId": "2347109331", "name": "Zhan Shi"}], "abstract": "LLM research-ideation systems aim for hypotheses that are both true and non-obvious, yet neither property can normally be checked. We build a testbed in which both can. Generators propose conditional interaction claims about two fully tabulated benchmarks, NAS-Bench-201 and HPOBench; every claim is verified exactly against the tables, and its non-obviousness is scored against a calibrated blind prior elicited from other LLMs. Excess truth measures how much more often a run's claims hold than this panel expects. Across a series of pre-registered stages with Claude and GPT generators, our main positive result is a simple two-stage step found by a harness optimizer: draft a large pool of claims, then select a subset in a separate call. For Sonnet on NAS-Bench-201, this step raised excess truth in every pre-registered test, including against a same-pool ablation that keeps only likely-true claims, without lowering precision. The gain comes both from credit for true claims and from avoiding near-miss false ones. In post hoc checks it held under every calibration, panel and truth rule we tried, and it exceeded random and type-filter selection from the same pools. Because kept claims recur across runs, the evidence weakens when runs and claims are resampled jointly, and it is not significant against Sonnet's own prior. Two secondary findings are also pre-registered. GPT makes real use of the results of cheap probes, beating shuffled and withheld controls, though not more efficiently than asking for more claims. Optimizing the harness for an LLM judge raised judge scores while sharply cutting precision for every generator."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3d1ff0065d0d0b2d11928c64d7953304b19080f5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.41216", "CorpusId": 295402364}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3d1ff0065d0d0b2d11928c64d7953304b19080f5", "title": "TinyAstral: Transferring Self-Evolving Harnesses to Tiny Generalist Manipulation Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41216, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2397760867", "name": "Ke-Hong Gong"}, {"authorId": "1753669202", "name": "Xiao-Xu Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2437476734", "name": "Guan-Ling Hou"}, {"authorId": "2525389666", "name": "Seung-Hun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2507875926", "name": "Meng-Qi Guo"}, {"authorId": "151506836", "name": "Run-Jia Tan"}, {"authorId": "2398983002", "name": "Zheng-Yu Wen"}, {"authorId": "2398589143", "name": "Qi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2274713895", "name": "Xiao-Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2397823150", "name": "Ming-Yuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "153122790", "name": "Michael Bi Mi"}], "abstract": "Robot agents built on vision-language models depend on both the planner and its harness: the tools, feedback, memory, and recovery logic around it. Can improvements to a large teacher's harness be carried into a compact agent with lower inference compute cost? We present TinyAstral, a two-stage approach. We first freeze an open-weight teacher and improve its harness through recursive self-improvement (RSI): frontier LLM agents read the teacher's trajectories and propose harness edits, and each edit is accepted only if it passes a paired statistical test. We then freeze the final harness, collect teacher trajectories with reasoning and failure recovery, and distill a small student that replaces the teacher in the same harness. On RoboTwin, harness evolution raises the teacher's success from 33.4% to 63.6%; the final 4B student reaches 62.8%. Across four harness versions, distilled 4B students score close to their corresponding teachers, and later versions yield stronger students, showing that harness evolution gains carry into compact models. The harness gains also transfer to other frozen planners without retraining. On identical episodes, the final 4B student outperforms open 4B agents, the open 27B harness-RSI model SenseNova-RoboRSI, and our own teacher model placed in three competitors'harnesses. It stays close to its teacher on all three training benchmarks, and on three benchmarks never used for RSI or training it scores close to a 552B planner running the same harness. On the measured deployment episodes, it uses about one fifth of its teacher's accelerator time."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "36ec15e8030a49e6304577ebecabb72ba5139fe4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.42141", "CorpusId": 295402632}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/36ec15e8030a49e6304577ebecabb72ba5139fe4", "title": "RoboArchitect: Automating the Design of Robotic Agents with Persistent Memory", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.42141, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2400645018", "name": "Sheng-Guang Wu"}, {"authorId": "2367283012", "name": "Su-Ning Huang"}, {"authorId": "2345417678", "name": "Cherie Ho"}, {"authorId": "2197078118", "name": "Ria Doshi"}, {"authorId": "2357770193", "name": "Hao Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2275638250", "name": "S. Yeung-Levy"}], "abstract": "Recent work shows that a frozen robot policy performs far better inside an agent harness, where control code decides when to query the policy, consult vision tools, or command the arm directly. Yet today these harnesses are built by hand and redesigned for each new robot or benchmark. We show that harness design can be automated, offering a strong alternative to finetuning on a novel setup. In RoboArchitect, a coding model observes a robot's rollouts, diagnoses failures, and iteratively revises the harness around the frozen policy. The harness evolves on a set of episodes for its optimization and is evaluated on held-out scenes and tasks. Without updating any model weights, RoboArchitect raises average success from 9.7% to 43.3% on unseen scenes (4.5x) and from 12.7% to 40.2% on unseen tasks (3.2x), across multiple benchmarks and policies. The gains are largest where the benchmark is far from the policy's training data: wrapping a general-purpose policy, the evolved harness yields a specialist that often outperforms even the finetuned version. Intuitive control strategies are automatically designed, such as lifting a jammed arm clear or reaching in horizontally when an obstacle blocks the way from above. The harness also evolves its own persistent memory, carrying learned experience, such as effective grasp depths, into later decisions. These results suggest that substantial headroom in robotic manipulation lies in the agent harness, and its design can be effectively automated and optimized without weight updates."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4379dc66d837b5ef0d5f346e2cc05ac9a5be8388", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38946", "CorpusId": 295373356}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4379dc66d837b5ef0d5f346e2cc05ac9a5be8388", "title": "MAS-Loop: Beyond One-Shot Orchestration towards Self-Evolving Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38946, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2260832033", "name": "He-Hai Lin"}, {"authorId": "2308550856", "name": "Qi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2383968687", "name": "Cheng-Wei Qin"}], "abstract": "Training-time multi-agent system (MAS) design seeks to learn reusable orchestration policies, yet prevailing approaches remain largely one-shot: an Orchestrator generates a system but only observes and learns from its terminal outcome. This paradigm discards execution trajectories that expose system-level failures and contain fine-grained information beyond the terminal outcome, leaving no direct basis for revising the source MAS, evaluating the updated MAS, or learning from the resulting contrast. To this end, we introduce MAS-Loop, an execution-grounded framework that closes this feedback loop by coupling an Orchestrator with a trajectory-conditioned Refiner. For each task, the Orchestrator constructs and executes a source MAS to obtain an outcome score and an intermediate trajectory. Conditioned on the trajectory, the Refiner localizes an actionable failure, formulates a trajectory-grounded diagnosis, and proposes a bounded patch to the agents, information flow, or execution configuration. The executable updated MAS is re-executed on the same task to measure its realized outcome under stochastic execution. This pair-MAS comparison provides complementary learning signals: the Orchestrator learns to generate strong systems with fewer recoverable failures, whereas the Refiner learns to expose and repair observed weaknesses, thereby driving policy-level co-evolution across training iterations. Experiments show that MAS-Loop achieves the strongest overall performance among the evaluated baselines, with consistent gains from refinement across all evaluated benchmarks. The co-evolution process also yields MAS-JudgeCorpus, a dataset of execution-grounded source-updated pairs with orchestration- and trajectory-level supervision, which can provide the community with support for MAS reward modeling and trajectory-aware investigation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6b354058e25ab0184a8cf84934bc09faaa8fdde7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36660", "CorpusId": 294524268}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6b354058e25ab0184a8cf84934bc09faaa8fdde7", "title": "The View from Nowhere: Population-Centered Agent Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36660, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2521792727", "name": "Shuo Yan"}, {"authorId": "2521582669", "name": "Ning-Ning Hou"}, {"authorId": "2326915783", "name": "Hao-Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2374041291", "name": "Hao-Chen Pei"}, {"authorId": "2520331106", "name": "Shang Gao"}], "abstract": "Self-improving AI systems can revise the designs that govern their own behavior. Yet existing approaches center improvement on selected parent agents and their evolutionary experience, leaving the broader population largely underused. We identify a substantial gap between population coverage (i.e., the tasks solved by at least one evolved variant) and the performance of the best individual agent. This gap shows that useful capabilities and diagnostic evidence emerge across the population without being consolidated into its leading agents. These observations motivate the view from nowhere, a principle for agent evolution: an agent should be improved by interpreting its behavior through population-wide patterns of success, failure, and prior self-modification. We instantiate this principle as Population-Centered Evolution (PCE). PCE distils task records and modification histories from the population into a population block, which captures recurring failures, complementary successes, and the effects of previously attempted modifications to guide improvements to a selected parent's harness. Under matched models, initial harnesses, and evolution schedules, PCE achieves 72.1% success on SWE-bench Verified and 96.7% on Polyglot, improving over a strong group-evolution baseline by 3.2 and 5.9 percentage points. In our evaluation, PCE reduces the gap between population coverage and best-agent performance by one-third on SWE-bench and closes it on Polyglot. These results suggest that treating the population as evidence, not merely as candidates for selection, provides a complementary principle for agent evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "189d26f30a4d3957d358f13ccc026bd2bf340164", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36469", "CorpusId": 294524519}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/189d26f30a4d3957d358f13ccc026bd2bf340164", "title": "CoHARL: Co-Evolving Agent Policies and Executable Harnesses with Reinforcement Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36469, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2409869810", "name": "Jian-Wen Chen"}, {"authorId": "2261083308", "name": "Peng Xia"}, {"authorId": "2371073432", "name": "Kai-De Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2283875337", "name": "Siwei Han"}, {"authorId": "2283868938", "name": "Zhen-Yu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2394170606", "name": "Charles Fleming"}, {"authorId": "2176884613", "name": "Xin-Yue Ye"}, {"authorId": "2378212884", "name": "Hua-Xiu Yao"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents interact with their environments through executable harnesses that govern tool use and feedback. Optimizing a harness for a fixed policy overlooks how its behavior changes during learning, while training a policy under a fixed harness leaves the interface that generates its training experience unchanged. This motivates adapting the harness throughout policy training. We introduce CoHARL, a population-based reinforcement learning framework that co-evolves a shared policy and a population of executable harnesses. The policy learns from rollouts across the population, and a proposer uses its execution traces to revise harness code during training. CoHARL achieves a 27.6% mean resolved rate on SWE-rebench, improving over harness-only search by 6.4 percentage points. Experiments on SWE-bench Verified and Terminal-Bench 2.0 show gains in software engineering and terminal-based tasks. Under matched training budgets, CoHARL also outperforms searching for a harness once before RL and alternating policy updates with revisions to a single active harness."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f79c920bed65caf56464c91858938b506b35643b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.37255", "CorpusId": 294621262}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f79c920bed65caf56464c91858938b506b35643b", "title": "Your Policy Is Secretly a Harness: The Decision Geometry of Agent Interfaces", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37255, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2356546712", "name": "Jian Mu"}, {"authorId": "2521426933", "name": "Rui-Bo Shen"}, {"authorId": "2382919045", "name": "Yao Shu"}], "abstract": "Language model agents are post-trained and deployed through a harness, the prompts, tools, and control logic that present a task to the model and execute its outputs. Harnesses are often revised and judged by how an already trained model performs under them, which assumes that what the model learned under one harness carries over to the next. We ask what a policy learns from its harness and what survives when the harness changes. Under KL-regularized control, we show that the log odds of an optimal policy recover how strongly its harness favors each action over the others, so a trained policy carries the preferences of its harness. Deployed return then equals the ceiling of the harness, the best return it offers, minus the mismatch of the policy, the return lost to its own suboptimal decisions. Training changes only the mismatch, while changing the harness moves both terms. Comparing harnesses with a fixed model therefore mixes what each harness offers with how well that model uses it. In MiniWoB++, post-training makes a model's preferences specific to its training harness. Reversing the order of the observation fields leaves every optimal decision unchanged yet lowers a trained model's success from 85.0% to 19.2%. Conversely, every new harness that a fixed-model comparison rejects but that raises the ceiling outperforms the original agent after further training under it, and ceiling and mismatch measured beforehand predict these gains. For coding-agent harnesses on Terminal-Bench Lite, ceiling minus mismatch estimated with a stronger model as a stand-in for the optimum reproduces the accuracy ranking, while either term alone does not. Code is available at https://github.com/2020-qqtcg/HRP."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3cf0eba004227d69d29f75378727730b84494914", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.37276", "CorpusId": 294621081}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3cf0eba004227d69d29f75378727730b84494914", "title": "Where Should the Evaluation Budget Go in LLM-Driven Agent Scaffold Evolution?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37276, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2395433933", "name": "Si-Chun Luo"}, {"authorId": "2143931690", "name": "Yi Huang"}, {"authorId": "2400611769", "name": "Mu-Kai Li"}, {"authorId": "2291976865", "name": "Hao-Chen Luo"}, {"authorId": "2290781543", "name": "Guanzhi Deng"}, {"authorId": "2381786074", "name": "Hai-Bo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2304518790", "name": "Jian Xu"}, {"authorId": "2378469378", "name": "Ze-Fa Hu"}, {"authorId": "2267281824", "name": "Jun-Lan Feng"}, {"authorId": "2395330519", "name": "Qi Liu"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM)-driven agent scaffold evolution improves agent workflows through iterative candidate generation and evaluation. Evaluation both guides subsequent search and determines the final scaffold, but evaluating every candidate on the full task set incurs substantial execution costs. We investigate how evaluation budgets can be allocated across these two roles. Our experiments show that sparse feedback can sustain productive evolution, while additional targeted evaluation improves final selection. Replay analyses further show that shared informative items and a common scoring reference support comparisons among closely matched candidates. Motivated by these findings, we propose \\method, an evaluation allocation framework that combines sparse search-time feedback with targeted final confirmation. A short fully evaluated warm-up establishes a shared informative core and calibrates a response model whose item parameters remain fixed during search. After search, \\method refits the model and applies paired successive halving, reusing accumulated observations and executing only missing evaluations. Across four benchmarks and two evolution frameworks, \\method achieves higher mean performance than full evaluation in most settings while using approximately half as many evaluation cells."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1639722492ef3f5ecb792f60ecbcc609edc7086b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36848", "CorpusId": 294621789}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1639722492ef3f5ecb792f60ecbcc609edc7086b", "title": "Evolving Agent Teams", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36848, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2265490669", "name": "Shi-Yi Cao"}, {"authorId": "2297406205", "name": "Zi-Ming Mao"}, {"authorId": "2265517766", "name": "Da-Cheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2308794329", "name": "Joseph Gonzalez"}, {"authorId": "2316149874", "name": "Ion Stoica"}], "abstract": "The rapid progress of software engineering agents drives interest in applying them to open-ended problems such as GPU kernel optimization. However, we find that existing coding agents converge to local optima on real-world, expert-written kernels (e.g., FlashInfer). We introduce Evolving Agent Teams, a multi-agent framework that escapes such local optima. It features (1) a central supervisor that diagnoses each local optimum and restructures a team of agents; and (2) a persistent file system that comprises a learning document, an evolution history, and per-team artifacts, grounding restructuring with accumulated experience. Under a matched token budget, Evolving Agent Teams consistently outperforms strong coding-agent baselines. With an extended token budget, Evolving Agent Teams generates a CUDA kernel that reaches 1.2x mean speedup over the production FlashInfer MLA paged decode kernel from scratch and wins on 36 of 47 production workloads from FlashInfer-Bench (0.86-0.99x on the remaining 11). It also demonstrates superior performance on tasks such as KDA prefill on AMD MI350X (3.01x over vLLM) and NVIDIA B200 (3.44x over FlashKDA), MoE expert-offload serving on Apple silicon (30.3 tokens/s decode for Qwen3.8-Flash-Next in MLX 4-bit on a 48 GB M4 Pro), and RoboDojo robot manipulation (62.1 on held-out seeds vs. 55.3 for the best leaderboard entry). Code is available at https://github.com/caoshiyi/EAT."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "24afe3b5b2a10d92b3aa9ecb57ffa09c40d55366", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35013", "CorpusId": 294366415}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/24afe3b5b2a10d92b3aa9ecb57ffa09c40d55366", "title": "Multiscale Policy Optimization for Long-Horizon LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35013, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2283841222", "name": "Tao Ren"}, {"authorId": "2156108232", "name": "Hui Yang"}, {"authorId": "2326669139", "name": "Jia Deng"}, {"authorId": "2267161179", "name": "Ting-Yang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2307111273", "name": "Qi-Yao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2283546526", "name": "Shuo Tang"}, {"authorId": "2349075584", "name": "Ziyang Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2218318364", "name": "Yu-Hua Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2479172209", "name": "Guang-Hao Li"}, {"authorId": "2334893634", "name": "Hua-Tong Song"}, {"authorId": "2511770988", "name": "Yi-Ning Li"}, {"authorId": "2257392203", "name": "Yi-Fan Luo"}, {"authorId": "2494836585", "name": "Kun Ding"}, {"authorId": "2344833453", "name": "Feng-Jie Chang"}, {"authorId": "2398802789", "name": "Chuan Hao"}, {"authorId": "2384120505", "name": "Ran Tao"}, {"authorId": "2346322341", "name": "Bryan Dai"}, {"authorId": "2265751832", "name": "Jia-Feng Liang"}, {"authorId": "1491631680", "name": "Jinyang Jiang"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon LLM agents interact with their environments through complete assistant turns while generating each action token by token. We observe that critic values vary primarily across turns, motivating optimization anchored at turns with finer credit allocation among tokens. We propose Multiscale Policy Optimization (MPO), combining Multiscale Advantage Estimation (MAE) and turn-level importance sampling to align credit assignment and policy updates at the turn scale. Extensive experiments across search, tool-use, and automation tasks with 4B and 30B-A3B models demonstrate consistent gains over strong RL baselines."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f7da3df3855759dd299cf7d2b05c8c3a3661af43", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36254", "CorpusId": 294523882}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f7da3df3855759dd299cf7d2b05c8c3a3661af43", "title": "Instant Software Factories", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36254, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-26", "authors": [{"authorId": "2155573042", "name": "Wen-Xin Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2292194581", "name": "Mikola Lysenko"}], "abstract": "Coding agents can quickly build prototypes, but a long tail of fixes, optimizations, tests, and releases separates a prototype from a product. A software factory handles this ongoing work through a repeatable process. We ask whether this work needs dedicated orchestration and how such factories should be judged. Given a tested repository and a coding agent, an instant software factory uses only two coordination inputs, a swarm of cron-job agents with standing objectives and a shared communication board, such as GitHub, where agents coordinate through the traces they leave. We developed a bootstrap prompt designed to set up both inputs from one round of human input, with governance as a separable layer. We distilled it from two manually configured industrial factories, observed for 15 and 17 days, and have not yet evaluated it. In the GitHub-coordinated CLI factory, daily merges rose 15x during agent rollout, an observed association. Its agents also filed 95% of new issues and authored 40 of 58 PRs that changed CI workflows, and at least 5.6% of issues marked as fixed were later found to have incorrect fixes. Unlike an agent scored on a fixed benchmark, a factory thus changes its own workload, evaluator, and measured progress. We formulate factory development as multi-objective optimization under this endogenous feedback. Over five months, we plan to evolve both factories, test the bootstrap on new repositories, and test five falsifiable hypotheses."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "24d9e11aba9169ff864082b0a892ac6add32a831", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35966", "CorpusId": 294524551}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/24d9e11aba9169ff864082b0a892ac6add32a831", "title": "EMMA: Structured Episodic Memory for Multi-Agent Workflow Synthesis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35966, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2288267095", "name": "Rui-Jia Niu"}, {"authorId": "2449597062", "name": "Kai-Wen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2253245279", "name": "Rose Yu"}, {"authorId": "2873011", "name": "K. Klinkner"}], "abstract": "Deploying LLM agents in specialized domains often require fine-grained task-specific procedures and knowledge a general model lacks, yet parameter training is costly and non-interpretable, and hand-engineered agent systems shift the cost to expert labor. Episodic memory updates at context level, but single agent adaptation struggles to alleviate cross-task interference, especially with complex tasks such as multi-application navigation or data pool analysis. Although multi-agent evolution alleviates this problem, existing methods lack clear definitions on update rules and rely on the backbone model to decide where and how much the system changes. This ill-scoped evolution makes learning follow the backbone's structural preferences rather than the observed failures: one backbone accumulates skills while another spawns agents. We present EMMA (structured Episodic Memory for Multi-Agent workflow synthesis), which treats the configuration of a multi-agent system (controller routing memory, specialists inheriting a generalist prompt, and per-step procedural skills) as episodic memory. EMMA makes the ownership and admissibility of updates explicit: a reflector assigns each diagnosed failure to the scope owning its correction and proposes one edit within it, and backbone-independent guards keep every accepted edit scoped, incremental and bounded. Across KramaBench, AppWorld and BBEH with five backbones and four baselines (ACE, GEPA, EvoMAS and ADAS), EMMA attains the best overall performance on every benchmark, up to 32.5% over the strongest baseline, maintains consistent improvements across backbones, and uses 17-38% fewer training executions than the most efficient baseline."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c113b04a15c1d4057d4b8f2777dd3f4b9e3a4f26", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34450", "CorpusId": 294367222}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c113b04a15c1d4057d4b8f2777dd3f4b9e3a4f26", "title": "EvolveMAS: A Unified Self-Evolution Framework for Decentralized Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34450, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2364789332", "name": "Ya-Lun Wu"}, {"authorId": "2357498174", "name": "Qi-Wei Ma"}, {"authorId": "2372232199", "name": "Bing-Bing Xu"}, {"authorId": "2266277013", "name": "Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2075398318", "name": "Long Bai"}, {"authorId": "2261851935", "name": "Bo-Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2259858493", "name": "Jia-Tong Li"}, {"authorId": "2382965039", "name": "Zhe-Qi Lv"}, {"authorId": "1429199889", "name": "Shao-Zu Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2118951553", "name": "Pei-Ying Wang"}, {"authorId": "2506267525", "name": "Jia-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2311074323", "name": "An Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2351809900", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}], "abstract": "Decentralized multi-agent systems (MAS) enable large language model agents to interact without centralized control. Agents need to adapt to other agents at test time and reuse accumulated experience to refine their interaction strategies for future interactions across cooperative, mixed-motive, and adversarial settings. However, existing approaches often develop adaptation mechanisms for particular interaction settings. This raises a central question: how can decentralized MAS support test-time adaptation and reliable experience reuse across interaction settings? We propose EvolveMAS, a unified self-evolution framework that supports experience-driven adaptation within and across episodes. EvolveMAS pairs each main agent with a dedicated MemoryAgent for test-time memory governance, which maintains isolated Structured Memory outside the main agent's reasoning context. EvolveMAS organizes adaptation into two complementary processes: Within-Episode Online Adaptation and Cross-Episode Knowledge Evolution. During Within-Episode Online Adaptation, MemoryAgent updates episodic, semantic, and working memory from interaction evidence to inform subsequent actions. During Cross-Episode Knowledge Evolution, the main agent uses memory and episode outcomes to distill reusable interaction knowledge comprising partner profiles and interaction strategies for subsequent episodes. Across five benchmarks and six evaluation configurations spanning the three interaction settings, EvolveMAS achieves the highest normalized average score of 77.2 among the compared methods. Ablations show that test-time memory governance and cross-episode knowledge reuse both improve performance. Interaction traces further illustrate how updated memory and distilled knowledge guide subsequent interactions. These results support experience-driven adaptation in decentralized MAS without updating model parameters."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "886a1998b96df61ee7459d584f766a54482d179d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34500", "CorpusId": 294366645}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/886a1998b96df61ee7459d584f766a54482d179d", "title": "CaliMem: Search-Efficient Data Agent via Calibrated Memory", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34500, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "1949334927", "name": "Han-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2329081678", "name": "Athan Z. Li"}, {"authorId": "2054842299", "name": "Fei-Yang Kang"}, {"authorId": "2130518057", "name": "Jia-Chen T. Wang"}, {"authorId": "2475498001", "name": "Yu-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "2357923277", "name": "Rui-Shan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2268321162", "name": "Ruo-Xi Jia"}], "abstract": "Data curation is a first-order design choice in machine learning, yet tailoring training data to a downstream model and task remains costly in labor, expertise, and compute. Datasets are therefore often processed through fixed pipelines and reused across applications. We show that this assumption is fragile: across 55 dataset--model tasks spanning biological domains, the same curation recipe can help one model while hurting another. Effective curation must adapt to the downstream setting, motivating automated search over data recipes. We first build an agentic system that searches for curation policies at deployment, improving data utility via \\textit{test-time curation (TTC)}. Although achieving performance gains, its search is driven largely by recent feedback and remains local. Adding persistent memory helps the agent retain evidence across iterations and reveals a deeper problem: it forms claims that extend beyond the conditions in which they were observed, sometimes abandoning an entire strategy after a single unsuccessful trial. To address this problem, we introduce CaliMem, a calibrated-memory agentic loop that requires the agent to predict the outcome of each proposed policy before executing it. Comparing this prediction with the observed result gives the agent a \\textit{reality check} on the claims behind its proposal. It uses the prediction error to retain supported claims, revise mistaken ones, and specify the conditions under which a conclusion holds. This makes the agent's beliefs testable, reduces overgeneralization, and supports more effective long-horizon exploration. Across biological and LLM post-training tasks, CaliMem consistently outperforms fixed curation baselines and published methods, and exceeds full-data training by up to 19.4\\%. Code is open-sourced."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bc1306df633b8067cea2f628202d8bbf58af2fdf", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33487", "CorpusId": 294227692}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bc1306df633b8067cea2f628202d8bbf58af2fdf", "title": "ReSight: Task-Driven Self-Improving Memory for Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33487, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2423412076", "name": "Li-Rong Che"}, {"authorId": "2473776641", "name": "Yang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2486727700", "name": "Yi-Meng Su"}, {"authorId": "34776599", "name": "Da-Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2354550156", "name": "Zhen-Yu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2507855939", "name": "Ji-Aan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2316673843", "name": "Yuzhe Yang"}, {"authorId": "2467100130", "name": "Xu Cao"}, {"authorId": "2345700914", "name": "Chuang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2430939627", "name": "Jiang-Feng Su"}, {"authorId": "2329880419", "name": "Xue-Qian Wang"}], "abstract": "Robots can use historical observations to answer spatiotemporal queries in familiar environments, but the presence of relevant evidence does not imply that existing tools support the operations a task requires. We introduce ReSight, a task-driven self-improving memory system for embodied agents that expands the capabilities for processing and accessing historical observations as task demands arise. ReSight retains keyframes and their spatiotemporal associations, separating observation evidence from extensible derived fields so that stored observations can be reprocessed for new needs. An execution agent and a coding agent share this memory through different interfaces: the former calls registered tools within a bounded online budget, while the latter uses a programmable workspace and a larger exploration budget to inspect evidence, write programs, and acquire models as needed. To realize capability expansion in ReSight, we develop Explore-Distill-Mount (EDM), which identifies capability gaps from execution and exploration records without task reference answers and converts exploration-derived solutions into reusable memory builders, query tools, and skills, extending memory representations and access capabilities as needed. Experiments on three public benchmarks demonstrate effective base memory access and capability expansion. With the execution model and online budget held fixed within each benchmark, EDM reduces mean position error on OC-NaVQA from 33.10 m to 24.72 m and improves high-level success on FindingDory from 38.3% to 63.8%. With the tools and skill frozen, success still improves by 28.3 percentage points on FindingDory episodes excluded from exploration and distillation. Experiments on real robot patrol memories further demonstrate reuse of these capabilities for subsequent queries and new memories, with fewer online tool calls on average."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "40008e6b7d8a029dd1b67ba09d7369ddd386d26f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33539", "CorpusId": 294228277}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/40008e6b7d8a029dd1b67ba09d7369ddd386d26f", "title": "CoEvolve: Counterfactual-Guided Co-Evolution of Memory and Collaboration in Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33539, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2521774953", "name": "Xu-Fei Shi"}, {"authorId": "2187417059", "name": "De-Rong Xu"}, {"authorId": "2260766940", "name": "Wei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2445224571", "name": "Yan-Yu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2227734260", "name": "Zhi Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2320264786", "name": "Tong Xu"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across diverse tasks. Yet their memory and collaboration architectures often remain static, constraining continued adaptation and improvement over time. Recent work addresses this limitation by evolving memory or collaboration based on interaction experience. However, updates to these interdependent components are not necessarily aligned: an evolved workflow may require experience that memory does not provide, while evolved memory may encode guidance that the workflow cannot use. To address this problem, we propose CoEvolve, a skill-centered framework for aligned memory--collaboration evolution. CoEvolve represents reusable local collaboration patterns as skills that link evolving collaboration decisions to their supporting memories. It further compares original and counterfactual trajectories to identify local evolution signals, which coordinate updates to the skill library, skill memory, and executor memories across rounds. Experiments on the ALFWorld and WebShop benchmarks confirm that CoEvolve achieves the best overall performance among the evaluated baselines, while ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of aligned co-evolution and counterfactual-guided evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "beebc1f82d57a7188e17bf6e2afd9a4f67c4e0b6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32253", "CorpusId": 293935928}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/beebc1f82d57a7188e17bf6e2afd9a4f67c4e0b6", "title": "RSIHub: A Composable Framework for Agent-Evolution Methods", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32253, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2282562956", "name": "Zi-Mu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2297250786", "name": "Xiaobo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2330241161", "name": "Si-Yu Ye"}, {"authorId": "2265797420", "name": "Jia-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2374373758", "name": "Si-Lin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2279412470", "name": "Yu-Ling Shi"}, {"authorId": "2512656473", "name": "Ruo-Bing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2518068418", "name": "Jing-Jing Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2356588864", "name": "Chao-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2286835859", "name": "K. Wu"}, {"authorId": "2339259770", "name": "Meng-Nan Qi"}, {"authorId": "2341532207", "name": "Kai Cai"}, {"authorId": "2374416817", "name": "Xiao-Dong Gu"}, {"authorId": "2266461235", "name": "Zi-Long Zheng"}], "abstract": "Agent-evolution methods use execution feedback to improve prompts, skills, tools, and orchestration, but their implementations often rebuild the same experimental machinery. We present RSIHub, an open-source framework for building, comparing, and extending these methods on shared infrastructure. Recipes compose reusable operators and declare mutable resources; RSIHub supplies execution, evaluation, recovery, and lineage. Researchers can focus on evolution policy while evaluation stays outside the mutable surface and results remain tied to candidates and evaluation conditions. We instantiate AHE, Hyperagents, A-Evolve, and GEPA without modifying the framework core, keeping method-specific logic in recipes and operators. We evaluate them with MiniSWE and Codex on Terminal Bench 2 and $\\tau^3$ Banking under fixed model and evaluator configurations. The largest observed overall pass@1 gain is 13.5 percentage points, while method rankings vary across settings and optimization gains do not always transfer to held-out tasks. RSIHub provides a common protocol for comparing evolution policies, inspectable candidate histories, and reusable components for developing new methods."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1374308cd138cb2f4bb0ff4e8739974da0b4f28d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32026", "CorpusId": 293935942}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1374308cd138cb2f4bb0ff4e8739974da0b4f28d", "title": "RSI-Opt: Recursive Self-Improvement Optimizer Agents For Model Training", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32026, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2195352476", "name": "Bing-Hang Lu"}, {"authorId": "2308106471", "name": "Yi-Ge Sun"}, {"authorId": "2364827101", "name": "Guang Lin"}], "abstract": "Conventional neural-network training typically follows a predefined optimization recipe, motivating a LLM agent that monitors training dynamics and adjusts optimizer settings as training progresses, but a fixed agent policy does not accumulate experience across runs. We introduce RSI-Opt, a plug-and-play Recursive Self-Improvement Optimizer Agents which evolves the knowledge that guides this process: a prompt, a playbook of procedures, a library of tested optimizer code, and a decision cadence. A meta-agent rewrites this policy using evidence from earlier runs, and an archive retains evaluated policies for further evolution. Across 5 generations on three training tasks, the meta-agents develop reusable procedures and code and conduct their own CPU proxy experiments. RSI-Opt substantially improves optimizer-agent performance, with representative raw-space summary $z$-scores increasing from $+10.48$ to $+14.33$ on the evolution suite with Opus 5.5 and from $-0.36$ to $+3.11$ on held-out tasks with GPT-5.5 through cross-model policy transfer. On the Burgers PDE, it reduces mean relative $L_2$ error by approximately 2.7-fold with Opus 5.5 and 8.8-fold with GPT-6.1 over the fixed-prompt agent baseline, although PDE transfer varies across LLMs and tasks. Controlled comparisons show that the evolved policy combines reusable optimization knowledge with adaptation to each task: initial decisions capture most observed gains on seen tasks, while continued adaptation improves outcomes on unseen tasks. These findings demonstrate how procedures and executable tools accumulated across training runs can improve optimizer-agent performance and support transfer to scientific machine learning."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4f2714079432936d86f0192f67bd7b2965d3dc6a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32813", "CorpusId": 293940253}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4f2714079432936d86f0192f67bd7b2965d3dc6a", "title": "HarnessMM: Recursive Self-Improvement for Harness Token Efficiency", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32813, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2345305718", "name": "Yu-Hua Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2509359032", "name": "Chang-Feng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2390234953", "name": "Fei-Fei Gao"}, {"authorId": "2299119265", "name": "Li Zhao"}], "abstract": "Agent harnesses repeatedly invoke reasoning after tool feedback, even when an earlier call has already fixed the next action. We introduce HarnessMM (Harness Minus Minus), a method for improving harness token efficiency through guarded decision reuse and recursive self-improvement (RSI). RSI iteratively refines companion harness configurations from training feedback, with validation selecting the configuration for deployment. A controller at the model-tool boundary reuses resolved decisions to replace later reasoning callbacks while actor weights remain fixed. Executable Decision Graphs store exact actions, literal arguments, causal dependencies, and public guards. Guarded Decision Reuse activates these persistent decisions from observed receipts; Certified Callback Substitution executes them through the native interface and records removal of the registered slow callback. New semantic decisions, unsupported evidence, and selector abstention invoke the original actor again. The representation restricts what feedback may change, while complete-task measurements establish quality and cost. On HumanEval, HarnessMM preserves Codex success at 90.82\\% while reducing Tokens/task (k) from 66.72 to 47.02 (29.54\\% saving). For DeepSeek Harness, success rises from 71.43\\% to 85.71\\% while Tokens/task (k) fall from 96.28 to 11.62 (87.93\\% saving). A separate MBPP ablation measures 26.91\\% lower cost than single-use graphs and 19.52\\% lower cost than retained callbacks. These results establish guarded decision reuse as a practical way to reduce repeated reasoning in existing harnesses."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "06cababf3ab1ce5ed336f106edf7cb9c7fc011b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32483", "CorpusId": 293940791}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/06cababf3ab1ce5ed336f106edf7cb9c7fc011b9", "title": "When Agents Fail: Model or Harness--Who's to Blame?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32483, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2116577679", "name": "Shao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2376371350", "name": "Hang-Fan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2308038006", "name": "Yuan-Yuan Shi"}, {"authorId": "2363724901", "name": "Hao Li"}, {"authorId": "2473419793", "name": "Min-Le Su"}, {"authorId": "2344931909", "name": "Kang-Cong Li"}, {"authorId": "2517990076", "name": "Jing-Ping Wang"}, {"authorId": "145468830", "name": "Chun-Jiang Mu"}, {"authorId": "2439283810", "name": "C. Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2378851357", "name": "Yang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2421192951", "name": "Bo Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2362971451", "name": "Shu-Yue Hu"}], "abstract": "LLM agents combine models with execution harnesses, so agentic benchmark performance reflects their joint behavior. We argue that harnesses should therefore be evaluated through their interactions with underlying models. We develop a framework combining five complementary measures: (i) Valid Completion Rate (VCR), how reliably agents finish normally and deliver evaluable results, irrespective of task correctness; (ii) Model-Harness Fit, how well a harness supports task achievement through valid completion for a given model; (iii) Cross-Model Fit, a harness's overall suitability across evaluated models; (iv) Interaction Residuals, pairing-specific execution strengths and weaknesses hidden by component-level summaries; and (v) Token Load, the token demands of agent execution, including failed attempts. We evaluate four general-purpose harnesses (OpenCode, Codex CLI, Pi CLI, and DeepAgents) with four core models, plus two fixed native model-harness systems, on six benchmarks covering context management, tool use, long-context planning, multimodal interaction, and artifact handoff. Including native references and stress conditions, 111 evaluation settings yield 52,168 trajectories. Harness rankings vary across models and benchmarks; task performance and valid completion can favor different pairings, and similar task scores can accompany distinct execution profiles. OpenCode ranks first in cross-model performance, while DeepSeek V4 Flash Preview maintains consistently high Mean VCR across harnesses on the shared four-benchmark panel. These findings support evaluating model-harness interactions, training transferable harness-use capabilities in models, and harness designs adapted to the model and task."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d1282dbd5a8137c8fcb1a08fa626a77c2940b284", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31062", "CorpusId": 293894765}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d1282dbd5a8137c8fcb1a08fa626a77c2940b284", "title": "SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31062, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2340040385", "name": "A. Mazza"}, {"authorId": "34490455", "name": "Elad Levi"}], "abstract": "Policy enforcement for LLM agents has shifted from single-turn content moderation to compound, application-specific policies evaluated over multi-turn transcripts and tool-call trajectories. Fine-tuned guardrail models are efficient but degrade on compound policies, and while LLM-as-a-judge approaches achieve higher accuracy, they incur high inference cost and tend to produce unstable predictions. Neurosymbolic methods offer a natural alternative, decomposing the policy into narrow neural decisions composed under explicit logic. However, existing symbolic layers lack the required expressiveness and are optimized against fixed data rather than the policy itself. To address this, we introduce SCOPE, which compiles a policy into an executable program of neural predicates executed by small models, deterministic extractors, and code that composes their outputs. The program is optimized jointly with its training data, using adversarial generation of counterexamples that target where the program deviates from the policy. We evaluate SCOPE on a benchmark of real-world policy tasks against prompt optimization, task decomposition, and workflow generation methods, achieving the highest average accuracy across tasks and models. Programs optimized with the weakest executor transfer to other models without further optimization. This allows them to be deployed in production on inexpensive models, where they reach accuracy comparable to frontier reasoning models. Unlike these monolithic models, SCOPE produces substantially more stable predictions, and each verdict is interpretable through the predicate outputs that produced it. Additionally, we validate the key components of our method through an ablation study. SCOPE thus enables reliable, interpretable, and efficient policy enforcement at production scale."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f3cde83293e92dbeb40df7b28da50ff3a45b6640", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31034", "CorpusId": 293894206}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f3cde83293e92dbeb40df7b28da50ff3a45b6640", "title": "CANNBot-CAKE: Collaborative Agentic Kernel Evolution for Ascend NPU", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31034, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2452537473", "name": "Deng Pan"}, {"authorId": "2516983810", "name": "Xue-Jian Gao"}, {"authorId": "2290318708", "name": "Jun-Yi Fan"}, {"authorId": "2452538545", "name": "Bin Du"}, {"authorId": "2479282931", "name": "Jia-Sheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2454210612", "name": "Xin-Xian Chen"}, {"authorId": "2516888290", "name": "Nok-Yan Chang"}, {"authorId": "2504354370", "name": "Xinlin Li"}, {"authorId": "2383066909", "name": "Ling-Chao Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2383854073", "name": "Jun Li"}, {"authorId": "2383326945", "name": "Yu-Wei Fan"}], "abstract": "Efficient custom operators are essential for modern deep learning workloads, yet developing high-performance kernels for Ascend NPUs remains slow, expert-intensive, and difficult to automate. Although AI agents now approach expert performance on GPU kernel benchmarks, current models remain less capable of generating AscendC kernels for NPUs, potentially reflecting the relative scarcity of relevant training data. We thus present CAKE, a CANN-native system for deployable custom operators that combines adaptive multi-DSL routing, separate Generation and Evolution modes, and reviewed knowledge reuse through CAKE Book and a Self-Evolving Agent. On 53 CANN-Bench v1.1 operators on Ascend 950PR, CAKE's aggregated results achieve $100\\%$ case pass rate on the standard set (1,060/1,060) and $98.28\\%$ on the hidden set (4,167/4,240), with an overall mean score of 78.63 out of 100 and an average performance speedup of $1.334\\times$ over the baseline. Evolution then pushes correct operators further: EngramGateFusion cuts aggregate device time by $46.6\\%$ over 20 rounds and roughly halves the mean roofline gap. These results offer a practical path toward closing Ascend's AI-for-kernel capability gap."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ae04a4539d0b3a9274e8115829a1cb47e06d0b39", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31709", "CorpusId": 293936006}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ae04a4539d0b3a9274e8115829a1cb47e06d0b39", "title": "Mining the Design Space of Multi-Agent Workflows", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31709, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "83579257", "name": "Xavier F. Cadet"}, {"authorId": "2313693827", "name": "Jun-Yan Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2327213146", "name": "Peter Chin"}, {"authorId": "46666605", "name": "Kyle Richardson"}], "abstract": "Designing large language model (LLM) multi-agent systems (MAS) still relies on trial and error and informal\"folk wisdom.\"Manual studies test only a few communication structures or topologies (e.g., sequential, parallel, shared-memory, or debate), while automated optimizers discard search trajectories after selecting a task-specific workflow. We present a two-phase pipeline for mining reproducible design principles from workflow code and execution traces. We repurpose AFlow's MCTS optimizer across six composable operators and 26 deterministically verified Enigmata tasks, retaining all intermediate rounds to build a corpus of 28,568 executable workflows. A sandboxed AutoResearch loop then mines orchestration code and prompt templates under an $L_0$-penalized validation objective and tests candidate principles on held-out searches. Across 300 feature-mining trials, we identify effects that persist on held-out searches across three levels of workflow design: workflows beginning with independent solvers and one-shot aggregation (Star-first) perform worse ($\\Delta = -0.045$); persistent shared documents (SharedMemory) outperform debate ($\\Delta = +0.135$); and prompt specificity and placeholder-based context routing yield gains comparable to communication-structure selection ($\\Delta = +0.118$). More broadly, our pipeline and results show the feasibility of turning discarded search trajectories into a statistical testbed for discovering and validating multi-agent workflow design principles."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d5eca343e925ce23d34e7522b4ba5511d985d7e6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31846", "CorpusId": 293936392}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d5eca343e925ce23d34e7522b4ba5511d985d7e6", "title": "Don't Forget What You Just Learned: Self-Directed Repair Turns Agent Mistakes Into Lasting Lessons", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31846, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2326838551", "name": "Yuval Kansal"}, {"authorId": "2314695668", "name": "Karen Ullrich"}, {"authorId": "2307071171", "name": "Polina Kirichenko"}, {"authorId": "2308102420", "name": "Ilia Kulikov"}, {"authorId": "152393000", "name": "N. Jha"}, {"authorId": "2249759469", "name": "Mohammad Pezeshki"}, {"authorId": "2334522785", "name": "Mark Ibrahim"}], "abstract": "Intelligent agents should learn from their mistakes so that experience leads to continuous improvement. Yet today's agents recover from a mistake only temporarily: in-context reflections are easily forgotten across sessions and compete for limited context, while full-parameter fine-tuning is expensive and can interfere with existing capabilities. We introduce Self-Direction, a mechanism for agents to learn from mistakes that combines the benefits of temporary in-context retries and policy updates. When an agent fails a task, it reflects on its own trajectory in order to extract a lesson and immediately distills it into a lightweight low-rank adapter. By keeping the original weights frozen, Self-Direction prevents adapter training from overwriting base-model parameters while training additional parameters equivalent to ~0.02% of the base model's parameter count for each adapter. The agent then saves these adapters in a persistent library of lessons, automatically routing them to solve related problems later. Experiments with agents based on open-weight models from the Qwen family across open-ended research (GAIA), tool-use workflows (Claw-Eval), and terminal tasks (Terminal-Bench 2.1) show that our method improves overall success rates by 8.9 to 17.0 percentage points and performs competitively with frontier systems. We find that Self-Direction reduces repeated mistakes across future encounters of a task, nearly doubling the reliability of an agent's corrections. Learned lessons also transfer: a handful of adapters generalize to dozens of new tasks without additional training. Ultimately, this self-directed repair transforms everyday mistakes into lasting lessons."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5c1de74ecd644cd617f56bed685607c2491651bd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29121", "CorpusId": 293844964}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5c1de74ecd644cd617f56bed685607c2491651bd", "title": "Recursive Self-Improvement on a Budget: Scoring Code Edits Before They Run", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29121, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "1402017423", "name": "Erfan Darzi"}, {"authorId": "2302641458", "name": "Sree Bhargavi Balija"}, {"authorId": "2510025844", "name": "Sheng-Feng He"}], "abstract": "A coding agent that rewrites its own scaffold has to choose which proposed edit to expand before that edit has been evaluated, and the search then has to return one agent. Each run fits one linear readout on the parent's steering-split solve rate and eight coordinates of the proposed diff, from 96 scaffold edits labeled by four evaluations of a 16-instance fit split, and then freezes the coefficients. HumanEval+ reserves 48 instances as the steering split and Polyglot reserves 64. That split scores parents and keeps an archive of four nodes. Huxley-G\\\"odel Machine selection draws a parent by Thompson sampling on the steering counts of nodes that have already been evaluated, and it expands the child that parent proposes. Every method then returns the earliest evaluated node whose steering-split solve count is maximal. The tables report the held-out score of that node on the other 100 HumanEval+ instances and the other 145 Polyglot instances, next to the root scaffold and the calibration edit with the highest fit-split label. Twelve runs of GPT-4o return a node at 87.0% on held-out HumanEval+, against 84.2% for the parent-rate ablation. On that split Huxley-G\\\"odel Machine selection returns 85.1%, the root is at 81.2%, and the calibration-best edit is at 82.8%. On the same split Claude Sonnet 4.5 returns 86.6%, beside 86.5% for Huxley-G\\\"odel Machine selection, 85.3% for the parent-rate ablation, and 85.1% for the calibration-best edit."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "39fbfb27515a932caa2ac9d9a723c1941b87786f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29317", "CorpusId": 293844861}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/39fbfb27515a932caa2ac9d9a723c1941b87786f", "title": "SpatialBrain: Spatial Intelligence Growing from Experience", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29317, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2384803761", "name": "Yi-Fan Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2349578127", "name": "Zi-Yang Gong"}, {"authorId": "2305627512", "name": "Xiao-Jun Ye"}, {"authorId": "2337824749", "name": "Yi-Fei Liu"}, {"authorId": "2448886444", "name": "Dong-Gang Li"}, {"authorId": "2155797490", "name": "Xiao-Long Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2266469531", "name": "Zhi-Hang Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2321523200", "name": "Xue-Ting Yang"}], "abstract": "Spatial reasoning requires more than access to geometric tools: a reasoner must decide when direct observation is sufficient and when to reconstruct, measure, or change reference frames. We present SpatialBrain, a framework that retains experience about these decisions while keeping its vision-language backbone fixed. The key distinction is between evidence about a scene and experience about how to reason with that evidence. 3DFusionMemory fuses current-scene observations into queryable spatial relations. A Spatial Skill Library provides reusable tool procedures, and Spatial Cognition associates task conditions with experience about when and how to use memory and skills. Completed trajectories are abstracted into process records and consolidated between task blocks, allowing later decisions to draw on earlier execution without transferring scene coordinates or reference answers. Across five spatial-reasoning benchmarks, SpatialBrain improves on direct answers from the same backbone on four and ties on the fifth. Component comparisons, experience-coverage curves, and traced resource decisions characterize how the framework accumulates and uses these resources."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c513d51f19a43f8d766413fdea60bf787d1b3e5e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29276", "CorpusId": 293844961}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c513d51f19a43f8d766413fdea60bf787d1b3e5e", "title": "Design and Tuning of Multi-Agent Systems Using Bayesian Evolutionary Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29276, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2501497044", "name": "Dominik Jehle"}, {"authorId": "2395769191", "name": "Tom Zehle"}], "abstract": "Designing effective multi-agent systems (MAS) remains a challenging and resource-intensive task. We propose a Bayesian Evolutionary Optimization framework for automatically discovering task-specific MAS topologies. The approach combines evolutionary operators to generate diverse workflow graphs with Bayesian optimization to focus cost intensive evaluations on promising candidates. In contrast to LLM-based candidate selection, our approach uses a graph-aware Gaussian Process and Expected Improvement to provide an explicit selection criterion during evolutionary search. We evaluate the method on BigCodeBench for code generation and on a tabular preprocessing benchmark using TabPFN. On BigCodeBench, evolved architectures achieve the highest overall performance among the evaluated agent systems. On temporal tabular datasets, optimized MAS preprocessing improves over the default TabPFN model on several tasks, while remaining competitive on IID datasets. Overall, the results demonstrate the potential of Bayesian selection for guiding evolutionary MAS search under expensive evaluation budgets."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ef12b85b1632958199fc5fc10f5445787cf5c375", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30042", "CorpusId": 293856715}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ef12b85b1632958199fc5fc10f5445787cf5c375", "title": "HGM-Dual: Lineage-Aware Guided Agent Harness Optimization for Long-Horizon Planning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30042, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2277299467", "name": "Sudipta Paul"}, {"authorId": "2003336037", "name": "Peng-Rui Quan"}, {"authorId": "2344642721", "name": "Aounon Kumar"}, {"authorId": "2261733814", "name": "Nicholas Tzou"}, {"authorId": "2283136782", "name": "Vivek Kulkarni"}, {"authorId": "1667831197", "name": "Srinivas Chappidi"}], "abstract": "Harness optimization, which improves an agent's prompts, tools, and workflows, is an increasingly effective way to enhance agents built on frozen or closed-source LLMs. Leading approaches such as the Darwin--Godel Machine and the Huxley--Godel Machine (HGM) rely on recursive self-improvement and were developed mainly for coding, where the competence needed to improve the agent coincides with the competence being improved. Planning tasks, in which an agent must produce a sequence of tool calls that jointly satisfies interacting constraints, break this alignment. There, open-ended self-modification repeatedly explores ineffective changes and consumes substantial evaluation budgets. We introduce HGM-Dual, a diagnostic-guided framework for budget-efficient harness evolution for planning agents. First, HGM-Dual represents agent evolution as lineage-aware context. This context records what each ancestor changed, which behaviors improved or regressed, and which modifications were ineffective, giving the editor explicit edit-outcome attribution instead of an unstructured archive of past agents. Second, HGM-Dual introduces error-conditioned dual-path child generation. For each selected parent, it generates a general child that preserves open-ended exploration and specialized children that target the harness components most likely responsible for the parent's highest-ranked constraint violations. HGM-Dual outperforms the strongest baseline by up to 14.45% relative improvement in case accuracy while matching HGM variants'final performance with 2x-4x fewer evaluation budget."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c6532750d7744506ece01c8d7daae9424b905e13", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30499", "CorpusId": 293894326}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c6532750d7744506ece01c8d7daae9424b905e13", "title": "Can LLMs Improve Their Own Harness? A Controlled Study on ARC-AGI-3", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30499, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2291593072", "name": "Jens Lehmann"}, {"authorId": "2327466746", "name": "Sahar Vahdati"}], "abstract": "A generally intelligent system acquires new skills efficiently without requiring humans to redesign its software scaffold for every new task. Yet language models rarely act alone. A harness is a symbolic program that orchestrates calls to the model, handling perception, memory, tool use, recovery, and inference allocation on the model's behalf. ARC-AGI-3 is an interactive benchmark in which agents must explore unfamiliar environments, figure out their goals on the fly, and keep learning as they go. On it, human-steered systems already report ceiling scores across the public games, so we know effective designs exist. We ask whether frontier models can build and improve such a harness on their own. Through a fixed coding bootstrap, each model constructs a complete task harness, acts through that harness on five development games, and revises the entire codebase twice. Every version is evaluated on twenty games that never influence adaptation. We measure task performance with Relative Human Action Efficiency (RHAE), which combines level completion with action efficiency relative to human baselines, and record dollar-denominated inference cost as a practical resource measure. Across 27 lineages, harness performance ranges from zero to near-ceiling scores. Two revisions raise mean held-out RHAE by only 2.80 points (95% interval [-1.44,7.35]). Development gains overstate held-out improvement, and reductions in inference cost can come at the expense of task performance. Human-steered systems demonstrate further headroom. These findings reveal a gap between autonomous harness construction and reliable adaptation under this two-revision protocol: even strong task performance does not ensure that subsequent revisions generalize or preserve existing capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bbaf552602d4c1b280790faa242e4616d5505590", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27531", "CorpusId": 293809074}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bbaf552602d4c1b280790faa242e4616d5505590", "title": "Automated Multi-Agent Organization Design: From Architecture Search to Organizational Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27531, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2354377707", "name": "Shao-Jun Xia"}, {"authorId": "2350419267", "name": "Yun-Er Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2307032631", "name": "Hui-Xin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2500267873", "name": "Di Wang"}, {"authorId": "2492070232", "name": "Yi-Fan Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2500961647", "name": "Liang Liu"}], "abstract": "Agentic system design is increasingly shifting from hand-crafted prompts and workflows toward automated search over agentic programs and interaction structures, with multi-agent systems receiving growing attention. Yet a central question remains unresolved: when does an automatically generated multi-agent structure exhibit a functionally meaningful division of responsibility? We argue that automated multi-agent design should move beyond searching for effective architectures toward identifying which responsibilities are functionally necessary, how they should be allocated, and when they should change. To explore this, we propose Automated Multi-Agent Organization Design (AMOD), which treats organization itself as a first-class design object, explicitly modeling how agent teams are composed, what functional responsibilities the agents assume, how they coordinate, and how collective decisions are made. We formulate AMOD as an execution-driven design loop. For each task, an automated designer instantiates an organization by assigning functional responsibilities and coordination rules to a set of agents. After execution, task outcomes, intermediate artifacts, and interaction traces are examined to identify which responsibilities may be missing, duplicated, or ineffective, and the organization is revised accordingly. We also propose testing discovered responsibilities by removing them, replacing them with generic roles, or changing their assignment under matched resource budgets, with complementary evaluations. AMOD provides a principled foundation for evidence-based and adaptive multi-agent organization design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c2ca147e72da9a3e963067b0002ef6af40650517", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27610", "CorpusId": 293809462}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c2ca147e72da9a3e963067b0002ef6af40650517", "title": "Auditing the Product, Not the Recursion: The Evaluation Evidence Behind Recursive Self-Improvement Claims", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27610, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2255142697", "name": "Zi-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "36737152", "name": "Di Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2233239462", "name": "L. Zheng"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) promises AI systems, such as self-evolving LLM agents, that become better at producing further improvements. Evaluating this promise requires separating product claims about the final system from process claims about the improvement process itself. Existing surveys specify the evidence these claims require, but how often studies report that evidence remains unmeasured. We examine this question through full-text coding of 114 eligible studies in a reproducible arXiv search frame frozen in October 2026, including 90 that describe their own systems as RSI. The codebook links claims to evaluation designs. Independent human coding, third-reader adjudication, and a separate blind coding of improver comparisons assess coding reliability. Among the 90 RSI studies, 90% report product evaluation beyond development tasks under a broad criterion that includes partial or unstated separation, while 60% report evaluation on tasks unused for selection. Only 22% audit two or more reruns of the full loop. In a human-coded random sample, the RSI studies show the same contrast: 45% evaluate on tasks unused for selection and 14% audit two or more reruns. Of the 26 studies claiming a better improvement procedure, 23 report a related comparison, but only 4 compare earlier and later procedures from identical starting artifacts at a matched budget. Across these results and a comparison set of established works, evidence for repeatability and improved procedures remains less developed than product evaluation. Three propositions explain how selection effects, shared evaluator errors, and changes in system state limit what product evaluations can establish. Together, the findings support a claim-indexed reporting standard that connects each claim to the evaluation design needed to test it."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eb7dc59898e4ee717481e6ef21c155eb83a68639", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27495", "CorpusId": 293809476}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eb7dc59898e4ee717481e6ef21c155eb83a68639", "title": "Self-Evolving Agents through Privileged Retrospective Distillation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27495, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2387206689", "name": "Yu-Hang Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2328023239", "name": "Yi-Fan Wu"}, {"authorId": "2405641662", "name": "Bo Peng"}, {"authorId": "2330281890", "name": "Ming-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2286338976", "name": "Jia-Yi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2323788930", "name": "Xiang-Jun Fan"}, {"authorId": "2340399373", "name": "Li-Zhu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2352021017", "name": "Zhuo-Kai Zhao"}], "abstract": "Self-evolving agents aim to improve from their own experience rather than remain fixed after deployment, but naturally collected trajectories often lack ground-truth solutions or verifier-derived rewards. We introduce Retrospective Self-Distillation (RSD), a multi-round framework that turns such experience into dense supervision using privileged retrospective context. For each on-policy trajectory, the same underlying model acts as both student and teacher: the teacher re-scores the student's realized actions while conditioning on cross-task precedents and lessons, together with turn-level reflection and future hindsight from the completed trajectory. The resulting teacher distribution is distilled back into the policy through on-policy self-distillation. Across rounds, the experience bank grows and the retrospective context is refreshed, allowing the model, its data, and its supervision to co-evolve. We further introduce frontier-guided context-policy evolution, where a stronger frontier model proposes constrained edits to how privileged context is constructed. Across ALFWorld, ScienceWorld, and SearchQA with Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-14B, RSD improves performance by up to 59.7% relative to the base model, while frontier-guided context-policy evolution adds up to a further 44.7% relative improvement over a fixed context policy. These results show that naturally accumulated experience can be converted into persistent model capability without ground-truth trajectories or verifier-derived rewards for the core policy update."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4529a4eb30cf72b9f37f1e96fcdaa2d7ed123238", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27435", "CorpusId": 293809157}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4529a4eb30cf72b9f37f1e96fcdaa2d7ed123238", "title": "Harness Self-Evolution for Long-Horizon Terminal Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27435, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328617416", "name": "Ru-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2249001715", "name": "Yu-Cheng Shi"}, {"authorId": "2375157272", "name": "Zong-Xia Li"}, {"authorId": "2403718571", "name": "Jun-Yao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2268636229", "name": "Zhong-Zhi Li"}, {"authorId": "2447743497", "name": "Yue Yu"}, {"authorId": "2238955130", "name": "Hai-Tao Mi"}, {"authorId": "2292161506", "name": "Dong-Ruo Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2422670781", "name": "Leoweiliang"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon terminal agents may solve a task in one run yet fail across repeated runs. Stochastic errors can propagate through actions and context, creating a gap between capability and reliable execution. We present RiseHarness, a framework for iteratively improving agent harnesses with model weights fixed. It focuses on flipping tasks with both successful and failed runs under the same configuration, using successful runs as references for failure analysis. RiseHarness combines agent trajectories with internal harness traces from a runtime monitor to examine how model actions interact with command execution, feedback, context management, and completion decisions. A Harness Handbook links this evidence to harness behaviors and source code, helping identify recurring problems in mechanisms shared across tasks. RiseHarness then searches other harness frameworks for relevant implementations and adapts them as design references to the current model and execution protocol. Candidate changes undergo behavioral checks and repeated task evaluation; versions with measured gains seed the next iteration. This loop integrates suitable mechanisms from different frameworks into a single harness and uses new execution evidence to guide further changes. We evaluate task success and reliability across models, ablate harness execution evidence and external research, and test transfer to unseen benchmarks with the same model. RiseHarness treats a specific model as the unit of harness adaptation, studying how changes to shared interaction mechanisms can make existing capabilities more reliable across tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "28eb658ee8628c31f486e0437df19309825ba26d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27889", "CorpusId": 293811680}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/28eb658ee8628c31f486e0437df19309825ba26d", "title": "Elicitation or Expansion? Demystifying Gains from Agent Harness Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27889, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2514761104", "name": "Younghyun Kim"}, {"authorId": "2311444501", "name": "Wooseok Seo"}, {"authorId": "2390411919", "name": "Minseon Kim"}, {"authorId": "2375289912", "name": "Seunghyuk Oh"}, {"authorId": "2345324748", "name": "Minjae Lee"}, {"authorId": "2445398643", "name": "Donghoon Kim"}, {"authorId": "2282471620", "name": "H. Koo"}, {"authorId": "2363686613", "name": "Jaehyung Kim"}, {"authorId": "2282472476", "name": "Wonjun Kang"}], "abstract": "Recent studies in harness optimization have shown substantial performance gains in language model agents without updating model weights. A well-designed harness more effectively leverages the underlying model's capabilities by shaping context, tool use, and execution workflows. However, na\\\"ive success rates alone do not fully characterize whether optimized harness yields more reliable success on tasks already within reach (elicitation) or a broader range of solvable tasks (expansion). This distinction is crucial for recursive self-improvement and evolutionary search, as expansion may provide a broader range of successful experiences to push the capability boundaries. Despite the growing significance, this distinction remains underexplored in harness optimization. We conduct a systematic analysis to study the effect of gains from harness optimization. Building on pass@k analyses in the reinforcement learning (RL) literature that compare models before and after RL training, we compare optimized harnesses with their pre-optimization counterparts. Specifically, we assess changes in reachable task coverage, compare harness optimization with model scaling and increased inference-time compute, and examine how gains vary with task difficulty. Across static prediction and dynamic tasks, we find that pass@1 advantages of harness over stronger interventions can reverse at larger attempt budgets and aggregate gains conceal shortfalls on hard tasks, underscoring the need for a fine-grained diagnosis."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8a82a8a048d8d66c6d70dc437a6ef671d40da17a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28380", "CorpusId": 293811987}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8a82a8a048d8d66c6d70dc437a6ef671d40da17a", "title": "Object-Oriented Harness: Evolving Agent Harnesses through Object-Oriented Design", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28380, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2350541241", "name": "Gao-Tang Li"}, {"authorId": "40135250", "name": "Bhavana Dalvi"}, {"authorId": "2321601631", "name": "Yifei Ming"}, {"authorId": "2258603407", "name": "Yan-Fei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2220095956", "name": "Zhongying CuiZhu"}, {"authorId": "71436125", "name": "Lesly Miculicich"}, {"authorId": "2253355959", "name": "Long T. Le"}, {"authorId": "2383302407", "name": "Rui Meng"}, {"authorId": "2136342754", "name": "Chun-Liang Li"}, {"authorId": "2359204202", "name": "Hang-Hang Tong"}, {"authorId": "2278969944", "name": "Chen-Yu Lee"}, {"authorId": "2305619900", "name": "Tomas Pfister"}], "abstract": "Agent harnesses are executable code that specifies the prompts, tools, and control flow around a language model. A single global harness can struggle with heterogeneous tasks that require different execution strategies. In this work, we introduce Object-Oriented Harness (OO-Harness), a harness optimization framework that evolves a hierarchy of executable harness classes using object-oriented design. During training, an evolution agent uses execution records to create a family of specialized harnesses, each implemented as a subclass of the base or an existing specialized harness via inheritance. At test time, a harness router uses routing lessons accumulated during training to select a specific harness for each task. Across three long-horizon benchmarks spanning business workflows, workspace tasks, and tool use, OO-Harness achieves the best performance among the compared methods, with an absolute improvement of up to 15% over the base. Extensive ablation studies examine the contributions of its core components."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c55aa369ac67549f7b4b13e3b405886a5d94da62", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28785", "CorpusId": 293845201}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c55aa369ac67549f7b4b13e3b405886a5d94da62", "title": "Measure First, Then Allocate: Calibrated Rollout Budgets for MCP Agents in Reinforcement Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28785, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2263121822", "name": "Hieu Tran"}, {"authorId": "1576489304", "name": "Zonghai Yao"}, {"authorId": "2335844854", "name": "Sunjae Kwon"}, {"authorId": "2261455807", "name": "Hong Yu"}], "abstract": "Training tool-using language models requires costly interaction, yet identical rewards within a rollout group provide no group-relative learning signal. We use reward disagreement to guide harness evaluation and the allocation of rollouts for policy training in Model Context Protocol (MCP) environments. We first introduce Discord, which tests harness edits using exact paired inference and decision-preserving early stopping: only disagreeing paired outcomes favor one harness over another. This insight motivates prospective allocation in BRACE (Bank-calibrated Rollout Allocation under Correlated Episodes). BRACE combines historical task evidence, uncertainty in task success rates, and a correlated reward model to forecast mixed-reward groups before allocating generation budget. Controlled harness experiments favor Discord, while live MCP experiments expose a retention-cost trade-off. At three Qwen3-VL model scales, BRACE exceeds the strongest plotted baseline peaks with fewer new training rollouts; historical-bank collection is counted separately. It also leads the compared methods on BFCL and NESTFUL without further training. Ablations probe the bank, estimator, and allocation score; prospective evaluation checks forecast calibration. These results support forecasting reward contrast before generation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9bd5e1f8e03de0a30b1853c6fda3299586dd89b1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03674", "CorpusId": 295506503}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9bd5e1f8e03de0a30b1853c6fda3299586dd89b1", "title": "ReVi: Recursive Recap Video Generation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03674, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505774015", "name": "Kyrie You Wu"}, {"authorId": "2245533308", "name": "Chao-Zhuo Li"}, {"authorId": "2367088216", "name": "Song-Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2163940136", "name": "Xue-Yang Feng"}, {"authorId": "2441627825", "name": "Ziluowen Luo"}, {"authorId": "2367092520", "name": "Chen-Xu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2405096448", "name": "Ji Qi"}, {"authorId": "2526388106", "name": "Jin-Fu Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2292634820", "name": "Li-Tian Zhang"}], "abstract": "Novels tell rich stories, but their textual form cannot directly present the sights and sounds of the worlds they describe. Film adaptations offer an audiovisual experience but require substantial production effort and viewing time; film recaps are shorter but depend on existing footage. Neither route directly provides a concise audiovisual account of a novel. We therefore study novel-to-recap video generation: producing a self-contained, source-grounded audiovisual retelling directly from a novel within a fixed viewing-time budget. This task requires compressing the story while preserving causal connections, allocating sufficient time to retained events, and aligning narration with newly generated visuals. We propose ReVi, a dual-loop multi-agent framework guided by edit first, generate second. Its Inner Loop plans story content, narration time, and corresponding shots before video generation. Across novels, its Outer Loop performs recursive self-improvement by revising both the editing policy and the meta policy that governs its revision. Experiments on two evaluation sets show that ReVi improves recap quality and duration compliance over representative baselines, with human ratings and multi-round improvement results providing further support."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7cf8f9618cb7e3d158965c0b29a17fee279938e9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25089", "CorpusId": 293807445}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7cf8f9618cb7e3d158965c0b29a17fee279938e9", "title": "DAGLoop: Aligning Method Design with Experimental Feedback in Automated AI Research", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25089, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2470954479", "name": "Dengyuxing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2360840966", "name": "Yong-Gang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2332787825", "name": "Qi Cai"}, {"authorId": "2438881144", "name": "Hua-Jiang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2355729616", "name": "Jun Song"}, {"authorId": "2371159496", "name": "Yang-Qiu Song"}, {"authorId": "2403462291", "name": "Yi-Ke Guo"}], "abstract": "Automated AI research agents aim to improve machine learning methods by iteratively proposing, implementing, and evaluating candidate methods. Existing work uses the outcome of each evaluation to determine how the next candidate should be revised for performance improvement. However, a candidate often combines multiple design decisions, and its overall outcome alone does not reveal which decisions produced their expected effects, whether their required conditions were satisfied, or which decisions should be retained or revised. Inspired by ablation studies in the machine learning community, we propose DAGLoop to test the expected effect of a design decision by comparing implementations with and without that decision while preserving the other decisions it requires. Specifically, DAGLoop translates each design decision's rationale into a testable expectation and pairs it with a targeted experiment, forming an experimental specification for assessing the expected effect under its required conditions. Using these specifications, we formulate ablation planning as the construction of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) from the candidate method's design: nodes associate decisions with tests, and edges encode dependencies. The DAG specifies which decisions each experiment must retain and supports joint tests when an expected effect requires several decisions to act together. Outcomes are recorded with the tested decisions, expectations, and conditions to form structured research memory. This memory guides the agent in retaining experimentally supported decisions, revising unsupported ones, and planning further experiments when the evidence is unresolved. Experiments on dozens of research tasks demonstrate substantial performance improvements achieved by DAGLoop across diverse executable research settings. Code and evaluation scripts are available at https://github.com/deng34567/DAGLoop."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dade126e1300171226187278f1b9aa4ea8e9ecea", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24380", "CorpusId": 293686538}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dade126e1300171226187278f1b9aa4ea8e9ecea", "title": "Train Locally, Learn Together: Distributed Multi-Agent Training via ADMM", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24380, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2350502165", "name": "Yi-Wei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2355718070", "name": "Chang-Sheng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2383170439", "name": "Yi-Cheng Lang"}, {"authorId": "2319604827", "name": "Qing Qu"}, {"authorId": "2311117948", "name": "Alfred O. Hero"}, {"authorId": "2332923951", "name": "Si-Jia Liu"}], "abstract": "Multi-agent systems (MAS) can outperform individual language-model agents by combining specialized roles, diverse reasoning processes, and collaborative decision-making. However, collaborative MAS training is typically centralized, requiring a common trainer to aggregate role-local trajectories and learning signals and maintain model and optimization states. This motivates a distributed multi-agent learning paradigm in which agents improve system-level capabilities through role-local learning and decentralized communication, paving the way toward scalable, resilient, and self-organizing MAS. To this end, we introduce, to the best of our knowledge, the first optimization-grounded framework for distributed MAS training based on consensus alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM). We formulate collaborative MAS learning as a consensus-constrained finite-sum optimization problem, naturally lending itself to distributed optimization via ADMM. The proposed approach alternates role-local learning with inter-agent consensus using distributed communication protocols such as ring all-reduce, enabling joint optimization while keeping each agent's data and training state local. We evaluate our framework on distributed safety alignment and general reasoning tasks. Under compromised-agent settings, ADMM can reduce HarmBench attack success rate (ASR) by up to 26.4%, while achieving reasoning gains comparable to centralized training. These results show that distributed MAS training retains the benefits of centralized joint optimization without requiring a centralized trainer."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0b091c0f4ded5f44d1b35dfc2966532ac8be063c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24454", "CorpusId": 293685727}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0b091c0f4ded5f44d1b35dfc2966532ac8be063c", "title": "MELD: Recursive Self-Improvement via Hierarchical Coordination for Multi-Agent Scientific Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24454, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2391757351", "name": "Si-Zhe Tang"}, {"authorId": "2293220962", "name": "Zu-Yuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2304029684", "name": "Ze-Yu Fang"}, {"authorId": "2391711013", "name": "Tian Lan"}], "abstract": "Multi-agent scientific discovery with large language models raises a coupled learning problem: research assignments determine both which experiments are pursued and the experiences from which agents adapt. We introduce MELD, a unified hierarchical test-time training framework that jointly adapts a team-level Coordinator policy and agent-level individual Scientist policies within a single discovery process. The Coordinator generates a joint portfolio of search branches and research directives over a shared derivation tree, while Scientists develop hypotheses and execution plans implemented by a shared frozen Executor. Both levels receive alternating GRPO-style parameter updates under a shared smooth best-of-budget objective, allowing team organization and individual research behavior to co-evolve in a recursive self-improvement loop. Our analysis establishes objective alignment for the hierarchy, characterizes conditions for coordination gains over independent parallel discovery and the first-order benefit of private adaptation, and derives a finite-budget regret bound for adapted models. On 22 MLGym and MLE-bench tasks, MELD is best on 20 of them and achieves the highest aggregate normalized scores on MLGym, using a 4B backbone for coordination and research. Most baselines use proprietary Scientists, and all systems share one frozen Executor."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "48d65c87a630337dc8c0376e1c6bc063b331d1ec", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24861", "CorpusId": 293804518}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/48d65c87a630337dc8c0376e1c6bc063b331d1ec", "title": "FlowMaker: From Tasks to Cost-Efficient Generative AI Workflow Deployments", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24861, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2341713401", "name": "Dimitrios Liakopoulos"}, {"authorId": "2279751412", "name": "Prasoon Sinha"}, {"authorId": "2397000", "name": "N. Yadwadkar"}], "abstract": "Generative AI workflows execute user tasks by composing sequences of LLM calls, where each call performs a sub-task (e.g., summarization or translation), forming a workflow. Executing such workflows requires decisions over workflow structure, model assignments, and deployment configurations, which jointly determine latency, cost, and accuracy under dynamic workloads and resource constraints. Existing systems optimize these decisions in isolation or fix them early, leading to suboptimal cost and goodput. We make two key observations. First, efficient serving requires jointly exploring all combinations of workflows, model choices, and deployment configurations under dynamic cluster state. Second, reuse of deployed LLMs is critical for reducing cost, but must be performed on-the-fly, as reuse depends on current load and resource availability. Together, these make selecting a workflow-variant-a combination of workflow, models, and deployment configuration-a time-critical problem. We present FlowMaker, which exposes a task-level interface and selects workflow-variants on behalf of users. To realize this, we design a constraint-guided load-aware pruning algorithm for fast, on-the-fly decisions. FlowMaker improves goodput by up to 10.14$\\times$ and reduces cost by up to 2.77$\\times$ compared to state-of-the-art baselines, including Murakkab."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22791", "CorpusId": 293453387}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "title": "Self-Evolving Coding Agents via Harness-Policy Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22791, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "122835755", "name": "Zhepei Wei"}, {"authorId": "2363427861", "name": "Wen-Lin Yao"}, {"authorId": "2257158371", "name": "Bao-Lin Peng"}, {"authorId": "2254643264", "name": "Dan-Qing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2339987832", "name": "Qian-Hui Wu"}, {"authorId": "2280195943", "name": "Hao Cheng"}, {"authorId": "145391513", "name": "Yu Meng"}, {"authorId": "2256752511", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents have shown promising potential in coding tasks, and their performance is jointly determined by the policy model and the harness that mediates their interaction with the environment. Indeed, harness and policy are mutually reinforcing and evolving only one component is suboptimal---a harness tuned for one checkpoint may not target the failure modes of an updated policy, while policy learning remains limited to the trajectories elicited by a fixed harness. In light of this, we propose Self-HPO, a recursive self-improving framework for coding agents that alternates harness and policy optimization, using the evolving policy as its own harness proposer. Specifically, in each round, the current policy first analyzes its own failures and proposes new harness candidates (H-step). The policy is then updated through reinforcement learning (RL) under the selected harness (P-step), and hard tasks identified during training form the next harness search set, keeping harness optimization targeted to the policy's evolving failure modes. Across three coding benchmarks, Self-HPO consistently outperforms harness-only and policy-only baselines, improving Qwen3.5-35B-A3B's average resolve rate from 57.8% to 64.8%, with gains generalizing to out-of-distribution settings. Further analysis shows that both optimized components retain gains even when paired with their base counterparts, while their joint gain exceeds the sum of their individual gains. Self-HPO also improves reliability (Pass@1) and expands the capability frontier (Pass@k) over the base agent. Additionally, we show that with a stronger external model (e.g., Opus 4.8) as the harness proposer, the same framework yields a further performance boost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d0e19b905f3897d4f84b8b9d1372e25b1abf6fd9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22915", "CorpusId": 293453167}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d0e19b905f3897d4f84b8b9d1372e25b1abf6fd9", "title": "Reward Engine: Reward-Centric Self-Evolution for Open-Ended Tool Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22915, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2507479416", "name": "Linda Feng"}, {"authorId": "2514570074", "name": "Ji Qi"}, {"authorId": "2515112941", "name": "Yi-Ran Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2374104171", "name": "Shao-Wu Pan"}, {"authorId": "2514718828", "name": "Xin-Yu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2514721164", "name": "Qi-Jun Li"}], "abstract": "Open-ended tool agents can complete the same objective through different tool paths, and their quality depends jointly on the interaction trace, environment state, and final artifacts, so such tasks rarely admit an executable ground-truth reward. The central challenge is therefore to construct a reward from existing experience that exposes the current policy's failures while remaining stationary during optimization. We introduce Reward Engine, which, like a self-motivated student, first works out what success requires and then optimizes toward it. From the task and initial trajectories, it builds a RewardPack of testcases for required correctness and rubrics for graded quality, each executed as a check that returns a judgment with evidence. The frozen RewardPack then guides the agent to revise and select its outputs, and can also serve as a reward for reinforcement learning. On WorkBuddy Bench Office and Claw-Eval, the constructed reward largely agrees with the benchmark graders, not reversing their ordering on 82.3\\% and 80.5\\% of candidate pairs. Used to revise executions, it raises three-run consistency ($\\mathrm{Pass}^{3}$) over Baseline from 45.0\\% to 54.0\\% and from 78.4\\% to 83.7\\%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "00f2980617c653bd7b470cb428980cb4389c41b0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23288", "CorpusId": 293546354}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/00f2980617c653bd7b470cb428980cb4389c41b0", "title": "CoEvolve: Self-Evolving Code Policies from Skills to Strategies for Humanoid Robots", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23288, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2514746381", "name": "Zi-Cheng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2119798830", "name": "Haochen Wu"}, {"authorId": "2514740104", "name": "Jia-Wei He"}, {"authorId": "2514851796", "name": "Su-Yun Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2454755594", "name": "Huan Yang"}, {"authorId": "2333875987", "name": "Cheng Chi"}, {"authorId": "2514966939", "name": "Hao Tang"}], "abstract": "Recent advances in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models and World Action Models (WAMs) have expanded the manipulation and whole body capabilities of individual robots. Yet a single robot remains limited by its embodiment, and many real world tasks require capabilities that can only emerge through cooperation. Existing multi robot frameworks largely emphasize task decomposition, allocation, and replanning, while providing limited support for adapting executable coordination when physical execution deviates from the intended behavior. We present CoEvolve, a framework that represents team coordination as executable code over fixed robot motor skills. CoEvolve introduces Verifier Guided Coordination Evolution, where a multimodal foundation model instantiates coordination strategies through motion primitives, semantic objectives, temporary robot roles, and bounded execution parameters. Physical verification evaluates each executed transition, rejected behaviors trigger structured coordination revision from an accepted checkpoint, and verified experience is retained to guide subsequent decisions. We evaluate CoEvolve with two simulated Unitree G1 humanoids transporting a freely supported rigid payload through constrained corridors. Across 15 base task runs with three multimodal backbone families, CoEvolve completes 8 tasks, with every backbone achieving end to end success. Additional evaluations yield verified placement on two additional corridor layouts and after both same-backbone and cross-backbone experience initialization, while exposing persistent limitations in motion precision and contact stability. These results support executable coordination as a practical interface for adapting long horizon multi robot collaboration under shared physical constraints."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "df4d31b3b58fd6e94c396dce6de6b129ad6312ca", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23470", "CorpusId": 293545984}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/df4d31b3b58fd6e94c396dce6de6b129ad6312ca", "title": "QD-Rec: Open-Ended Evolution of Recommendations via Quality-Diversity", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23470, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2069409929", "name": "Takashi Ishida"}, {"authorId": "2099390928", "name": "Taro Makino"}, {"authorId": "2310230616", "name": "Issa Sugiura"}], "abstract": "Open-ended search combined with foundation models has produced advances such as faster algorithms, new results on open mathematical problems, and self-improving agents. We extend this paradigm to recommendation for decision-making, focusing on decisions where the set of possible choices is fixed but the reasons for choosing them are not. In high-stakes decisions, e.g., which company to acquire or which customer to target, comparing diverse, well-reasoned options can be more useful than a single ranked list. We formalize open-ended recommendation as building a diverse, high-quality set of recommendations, each an item together with a hypothesis, a natural-language reason why the item fits the needs of a decision maker. We propose QD-Rec, a quality--diversity method that selects items with a contextual bandit and generates hypotheses with a foundation model from earlier ones kept in an archive as stepping stones. A new recommendation enters the archive if it is novel or better than its nearest neighbor. Across M&A, customer targeting, and paper and book recommendation, QD-Rec gives the highest mean quality and builds the largest archive in most settings."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "662e9bd962d801a390a02ebf868bfb15575eb28d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22561", "CorpusId": 293453025}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/662e9bd962d801a390a02ebf868bfb15575eb28d", "title": "FRACTAL: A Self-Similar Multi-Agent Orchestration Architecture with Formal Communication, Convergence, and Depth Bounds", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22561, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "10122218", "name": "Ohad Dan"}, {"authorId": "2514439267", "name": "Abhinav Taliyan"}], "abstract": "We present FRACTAL, a self-similar multi-agent architecture for recursive goal decomposition, in which the same five-component organizational pattern -- Orchestrator, Ensemble, Knowledge Ledger, Decision Maker, Meta-Observer -- recurs at every level of a decomposition tree. We make four formal contributions: (1) a communication bound showing intra-node cost is linear in ensemble size and round count (O(kr)) under a two-phase blind-proposal/stress-test protocol, versus quadratic cost for free-form debate, with an explicit, stated assumption on fixed evaluator count; (2) a two-mode orchestration criterion separating generative and evaluative phases via a measurable diversity-saturation threshold; (3) a supermajority convergence guarantee with explicit probability bounds at stated agreement levels, and a formal depth bound under an operationalized goal-complexity measure; (4) a Bayesian inter-layer escalation mechanism unifying hysteresis and contract-breach signaling under one posterior update. We illustrate the architecture's execution qualitatively on a worked example and a multi-layer case study; we do not report quantitative performance claims in this paper, and we explicitly discuss the formal and inductive-bias limitations of the model, including an unimplemented but architecture-compatible direction toward bounded self-improvement. We use\"fractal\"as an architectural metaphor for self-similarity, not a measured mathematical property. Empirical validation of the architecture's comparative performance is left to future work."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3088b25b3c0489f29cd67a55e16bc7869bb035c5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21218", "CorpusId": 292982299}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3088b25b3c0489f29cd67a55e16bc7869bb035c5", "title": "AutoOnboarder: Autonomously Onboarding LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21218, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2125483441", "name": "Tanay Arora"}, {"authorId": "2487088168", "name": "Cong-Lin Liu"}], "abstract": "Agentic large language models have been documented to lose reasoning capability when placed inside a harness, the software that mediates their observations and actions. To reconcile these losses, we introduce AutoOnboarder, an autonomous system that adapts an agent to a task the way a trainer onboards a new employee. It places the agent under the supervision of an intelligent ``Onboarder''coding agent, which iteratively tailors its foundation model, prompt, and harness source code to simultaneously improve performance and decrease cost. Like a real onboarding process, the Onboarder is only given tens of examples, and all evaluation data is isolated on a separate virtual machine to prevent leakage. We report the lessons learned from building and testing AutoOnboarder on genomic-sequencing prior authorization form submission. Autonomously onboarded agents are able to match or exceed the performance of a Gemini 3.5 Flash baseline at up to 29$\\times$ lower costs. However, these agents remain imperfect, and the Onboarding process varies greatly in terms of final performance. We conclude that future Onboarding systems require a robust environment for the Onboarder, including a structured thought interface, (controlled) internet access, and a wide model search space."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d017d7888d16683c5109c744494f88110b55c050", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21887", "CorpusId": 293003845}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d017d7888d16683c5109c744494f88110b55c050", "title": "MetaShare: Failure-Aware Collective Self-Improvement for Open-Ended Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21887, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2049035448", "name": "Vishal Bhutani"}, {"authorId": "2003680911", "name": "Prasang Gupta"}, {"authorId": "2444865616", "name": "Sumanth Chundru"}, {"authorId": "2054437310", "name": "K. Paul"}], "abstract": "Humans often learn fastest not by repeating success, but by remembering which failures not to repeat. Open-ended self-improving agents can share experience across lineages, as in GEA, or edit their own improvement mechanisms, as in DGM-Hyperagents, yet failed improvement attempts are rarely retained as reusable cross-branch knowledge, and search can collapse onto redundant strategies. We introduce MetaShare, a failure-aware framework that records successful and unsuccessful agent modifications together with signed performance deltas. Before each generation, MetaShare retrieves diverse high-performing strategies alongside high-confidence failures from other lineages, letting agents reuse effective mechanisms while avoiding known dead ends. A task-blind auditor periodically injects absent or underrepresented mechanism categories, while embedding-based de-ranking limits redundancy during retrieval. Multiple meta-agents propose modifications in parallel at heterogeneous temperatures behind a deterministic selector that gates applicability, scope, compilability, and size. Experiments across paper review, coding, math grading, robotics reward design, and financial audit-risk assessment show that MetaShare's median matches or exceeds DGM-Hyperagents'final performance and reaches its own best-performing agent in 1.7--2.9$\\times$ fewer evolution iterations."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f55d7bbbefc318ff2cd83c8ef3e02eeb8ad0adfe", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22194", "CorpusId": 293003667}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f55d7bbbefc318ff2cd83c8ef3e02eeb8ad0adfe", "title": "AutoSFT: An Agentic System for Automated Data Research in Supervised Fine-Tuning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22194, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2171127494", "name": "Bing-Xiang He"}, {"authorId": "2510616348", "name": "Peng Sun"}, {"authorId": "151474865", "name": "Shi-Hao Peng"}, {"authorId": "2509358622", "name": "Shao-Xuan Li"}, {"authorId": "2114239499", "name": "Hu Xiao"}, {"authorId": "2511809677", "name": "Hong-Jian Shi"}, {"authorId": "2354260586", "name": "Zi-Xuan Fu"}, {"authorId": "2273566018", "name": "Zi-Qing Qiao"}, {"authorId": "2329372501", "name": "Yu-Xin Zuo"}, {"authorId": "2353956974", "name": "Cheng Qian"}, {"authorId": "2506258395", "name": "Xin Yu"}, {"authorId": "2510633242", "name": "Jia-Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2356578188", "name": "Xiu-Si Chen"}, {"authorId": "2512063602", "name": "Yi-Zhen Song"}, {"authorId": "2332078118", "name": "Xiang-Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510537266", "name": "Iofai Lei"}, {"authorId": "2312864296", "name": "Yu-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2284766400", "name": "Ning Ding"}, {"authorId": "2316519794", "name": "Zhi-Yuan Liu"}, {"authorId": "48631777", "name": "Xiaozhi Wang"}, {"authorId": "51131083", "name": "Chao-Jun Xiao"}], "abstract": "Language model agents increasingly automate post-training, but when given the full training stack they converge on supervised fine-tuning and spend most effort revising training data. Existing systems give the agent one kind of control, selecting rows from a fixed pool or calling a generator, whereas a human data engineer adjusts proportions, prunes, repairs, replaces and synthesizes the data. We design a data action space for these changes and ask whether an automated system can make the full range rather than one of them, and what structure that requires. We first run this loop with a single agent, and it reaches only one action, adjusting category proportions, while its context fills with execution output and is discarded mid-search. We therefore introduce \\textbf{AutoSFT}, a multi-agent system where a meta agent separates execution from analysis, gathers proposals from independent angles, and calls a synthesis subagent for capabilities the pool cannot cover, with the model, the trainer, and the budget fixed within each comparison. Adding each component in turn helps: the executor halves context loss, the analysis subagents lift the best IFEval by $+6.5$, and synthesis adds $+3.4$ IFEval and $+2.9$ IFBench. The same loop transfers to code, where analysis adds $+3.7$ MBPP+ over a single agent that does not improve. Every result comes from runs that retrain from the base model each round, so each component's effect is individually attributable. Our findings suggest that automating SFT data research depends more on covering diverse data actions than on a stronger single action."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d2232ea220a6edd87649e010883185a67e35f2b2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22736", "CorpusId": 293452835}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d2232ea220a6edd87649e010883185a67e35f2b2", "title": "Spatial Memory by Agents, for Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22736, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2303431191", "name": "Qian-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2453694994", "name": "Ce Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2279989840", "name": "Zhan-Peng Luo"}, {"authorId": "2514438560", "name": "Peng-Peng Su"}, {"authorId": "2402763425", "name": "Shuo Yang"}, {"authorId": "2514798306", "name": "Hao Xu"}], "abstract": "Spatial memory enables robots to reuse past observations for localization, navigation, and question answering. Designing such memory involves jointly deciding what to retain and how to retrieve it for diverse semantic, metric, and temporal needs. We investigate whether coding agents can automate this design process through iterative implementation and evaluation. Under a fixed development budget, a Designer Agent writes and revises code for memory construction and retrieval using feedback from a fixed Query Agent. The resulting implementations are frozen before building memories from held-out observation histories, without access to downstream questions, and evaluated across five complementary task tracks using the same Query Agent. Across three independent design searches, Codex / GPT-6 Astra averages 44.23 on the equally weighted five-track score, versus 29.32 for ReMEmbR, the strongest evaluated baseline supporting all five tracks, a 50.9% relative improvement. Qualitative analysis relates performance differences to evidence retention and retrieval, while identifying grounding and evidence-use failures. These results demonstrate the potential of coding agents to automate spatial memory design, while revealing task-dependent trade-offs in performance and computational cost. Project page: https://ba-fa-3344.github.io/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eccc0716bef086e99e5430e9b4c72fcd3d817e60", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22466", "CorpusId": 293453108}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eccc0716bef086e99e5430e9b4c72fcd3d817e60", "title": "Harness-Delta Attribution: Dissecting Gains From Harness Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22466, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2357365368", "name": "Wen-Xuan Ding"}, {"authorId": "2365376107", "name": "Qin Lu"}, {"authorId": "2302894470", "name": "Chang-Long Yu"}, {"authorId": "2405778950", "name": "Sha Li"}, {"authorId": "2244738638", "name": "Shuo-Wei Jin"}, {"authorId": "2362222390", "name": "Xin Liu"}, {"authorId": "2408193613", "name": "Zhen-Yu Shi"}, {"authorId": "2271320260", "name": "Greg Durrett"}], "abstract": "Harness evolution automatically optimizes the prompts, skills, tools, and orchestration code around a fixed-weight model, offering a way to improve agentic systems without updating model weights. The agent harness is iteratively optimized to achieve higher performance on the search set. But how much of this gain reflects benchmark-specific overfitting or additional inference-time compute, and how much may generalize beyond the search set? In this work, we introduce Harness-Delta Attribution (HDA), an evaluation protocol that dissects an evolution gain into artifact-driven overfitting, gains explained by test-time scaling, and the remainder, which gives an upper bound on residual generalizable improvement. We apply HDA to 11 executor models and three harness-evolution frameworks across math, coding, creativity, and agentic tasks. In 21 of these settings that show statistically significant search-set gains, we find that a large share of the improvement is explained by overfitting or test-time scaling. Specifically, among these 21 settings, overfitting and test-time scaling account for 54% and 14% of the gain on average; their combined share exceeds 50% in 14 settings. The residual and held-out transfer vary substantially across tasks, models, and frameworks. Importantly, search-set gains often fail to transfer to held-out tasks, and provide limited evidence that they will persist under practical distribution shifts. Moreover, validation-based signals are insufficient to prevent overfitting. These results motivate attribution-aware evaluation in future harness evolution research."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d37672893b81fe88028ade1f4166a77a037eeee2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19424", "CorpusId": 292902687}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d37672893b81fe88028ade1f4166a77a037eeee2", "title": "VideoHarness-SRSI: Structured Recursive Self-Improvement for Video Understanding Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19424, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2254334449", "name": "Zi-Hao Lin"}, {"authorId": "2309801263", "name": "Hao-Yu Dong"}, {"authorId": "2379955487", "name": "Hao-Bo Xu"}, {"authorId": "2339005997", "name": "Chi-Chih Chang"}, {"authorId": "2266396687", "name": "Xu-Ying Ning"}, {"authorId": "2258547648", "name": "Ying-Long Xia"}, {"authorId": "2504318197", "name": "Shuang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2352021017", "name": "Zhuo-Kai Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2507464273", "name": "Xin-Yi Song"}, {"authorId": "1995922397", "name": "Dongfang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2293354444", "name": "Li-Fu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2393886238", "name": "Yang Song"}, {"authorId": "2266712798", "name": "Qi-Fan Wang"}], "abstract": "LLM system capabilities depend not only on model parameters but also on the executable harnesses that organize information acquisition, tool use, reasoning, and memory. Recursive harness evolution can automate their design, but how it can reliably discover generalizable systems from limited, noisy, and repeatedly reused feedback remains unclear. We study this question in agentic video understanding, where rich multimodal evidence and coupled mechanisms make harness optimization challenging. We first introduce VHarness-BRSI, a recursive self-improvement framework that evolves video harnesses around a frozen vision-language model. Analyzing over 4,000 evolved candidates, we find it competitive with hand-engineered systems but identify three interconnected failure modes: validation overfitting, search-budget dilution, and the early-experience trap. We trace all three to a common root: the same imperfect validation evidence drives both harness revisions and the proposer's search strategy. We therefore propose VHarness-SRSI, an evidence-governed framework in which every search decision rests on evidence that is transferable, sufficient, and gathered before the search commits. It restricts the proposer to content-free feedback, informs exploration with instrumented warm-up experiments, and develops mechanisms in isolated probe-race branches before factorial composition and joint refinement. Across four video understanding benchmarks, VideoHarness-SRSI improves the equally weighted mean held-out score by 6.2% relative to VideoHarness-BRSI and reduces the mean validation-test gap by 37.9%. Reliable harness evolution thus depends on how search is organized and informed, not only on how well it optimizes validation performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19951", "CorpusId": 292903495}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "title": "AtomSearch: Evidence-Grounded Tree Search for Agent Skill Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19951, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2497901572", "name": "Jiang-Shuai-Chen-Rui-,-Zhang-Rong-,-Wang-Gui-Fang- Tong"}, {"authorId": "2430689890", "name": "Sen Deng"}, {"authorId": "2469306138", "name": "Ming-Pei Peng"}, {"authorId": "2311866886", "name": "Jian-Feng Pan"}], "abstract": "Agent skills adapt frozen models through editable external instructions. Revising several rules at once makes changes difficult to inspect, while judging each local edit only by its immediate score can discard useful intermediate states. We introduce AtomSearch, which keeps revision actions local while using subsequent skill states to guide search. Contrastive atomic editing links each primitive rule edit to failed and successful executions, a behavioral hypothesis, and an intended scope. Skill-State Tree Search (STS) evaluates short edit compositions and selectively retains them as executable states that can be revisited and extended. Outcome memory supplies later proposals with observed repair and regression statistics. We evaluate AtomSearch across four tasks and five target models. With deepseek-v4-flash, it exceeds SkillOpt on three of four tasks, including a 9.68-percentage-point gain on LiveMathematicianBench, and SkillOpt's final skills contain 2.3-19.7 times as many characters. In 67% of completed searches, the best validation state passes through an ancestor that was not highest-scoring at its depth. These results show how local rule edits can support the exploration of useful revision paths and yield compact skills, with additional offline evaluations used to explore alternative states."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b16b80a648553146a8be8f184ef112a3cb0e17a6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23726", "CorpusId": 293546508}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b16b80a648553146a8be8f184ef112a3cb0e17a6", "title": "RedDarwin: Evolving Jailbreak Skills against Frontier LLMs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23726, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2458321075", "name": "Zhou-Yuan Ma"}, {"authorId": "2374911264", "name": "Yu-Tao Wu"}, {"authorId": "1753845931", "name": "Hanxun Huang"}, {"authorId": "2328107098", "name": "Xiang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2340824747", "name": "Yi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2387879011", "name": "Xing-Jun Ma"}], "abstract": "Red teaming must continually adapt as frontier LLM safeguards evolve, yet existing approaches largely search for adversarial prompts or optimize within fixed attack procedures. We observe that even when an attack becomes ineffective, its underlying mechanisms can remain useful and can be revised or recombined to construct stronger attacks. Based on this insight, we introduce RedDarwin, an agentic framework that shifts the unit of jailbreak evolution from prompts and fixed attacks to executable attack skills. RedDarwin consists of 47 reusable base skills and autonomously revises, composes, executes, and selects them using target-model feedback, allowing successful discoveries to become building blocks for subsequent evolution. RedDarwin produces 65 evolved skills, improving 44 of 47 base attacks and increasing their mean attack success rate (ASR) on AdvBench from 18.8% to 47.3%. These evolved skills consistently outperform baselines on the held-out StrongREJECT and frontier LLMs unseen during evolution; with only 10 target-model queries, RedDarwin achieves 100% ASR on GPT-5.6-Sol, 92% on Claude-Sonnet-5, and 80% on Gemini-3.8-Flash. Analysis of the evolution trajectories further shows that strong attacks often emerge from novel combinations of reusable mechanisms rather than from individually dominant ones. We believe this paradigm can accelerate adaptive red teaming and inform stronger safeguards, creating complementary value for both safety evaluation and defense."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dcc2fbfbc8fdb2f8c8b0b29d6bb08bbc4e3933c7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18813", "CorpusId": 292894673}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dcc2fbfbc8fdb2f8c8b0b29d6bb08bbc4e3933c7", "title": "Self-Evolving Agent Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18813, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2174671074", "name": "Chu-Yang Wei"}, {"authorId": "2333357871", "name": "Hao-Xiang Guan"}, {"authorId": "2203918573", "name": "Ming-Hang Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2434954462", "name": "Ken Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2367749242", "name": "Ji-Yan He"}, {"authorId": "46223995", "name": "Xi-Ren Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2468000469", "name": "Huan-Huan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2461908277", "name": "Yi-Fei Shen"}], "abstract": "Harness self-evolution revises an agent's software scaffolding, including prompts, memory, workflows, and executable components, while keeping model weights fixed. We formalize a feedback-driven process in which accepted revisions persist across runs. The framework separates update targets from proposal and admission mechanisms. It distinguishes changes to interpreted state from revisions to tools and code, repository-level control logic, and plugin composition. We characterize search through evolutionary optimization and examine its reliability through continual PAC-learning. Under explicit assumptions, the analysis yields an expected generalization bound, a retention bound conditional on historical regression budgets, and a high-probability admission rule using fresh, independent validation data. We examine how persistent harness revisions affect knowledge sharing and organizational workflows, with implications for scientific reliability, physical safety, and institutional accountability. Cases from prior work illustrate model--harness co-design, distributed execution, and plugin reuse. Reliable deployment requires continual validation as task distributions and evaluators change, with independent safeguards for learning, runtime privileges, and consequential actions. We provide a comprehensive 66-page discussion of related work, theory, algorithms, evaluation, applications, and the broader impact of Harness RSI, together with continuously updated code and supplementary materials, at https://github.com/zgcagi/Harness-RSI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d0da293bdf2aebff1ff751baabf4d94685436872", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17613", "CorpusId": 292896378}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d0da293bdf2aebff1ff751baabf4d94685436872", "title": "MAS-Gate: Unlocking Multi-Agent System Lifecycle via Node-Centric Quantification", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17613, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2260832033", "name": "He-Hai Lin"}, {"authorId": "2342357003", "name": "Zi-Xuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510560117", "name": "Ming Yan"}, {"authorId": "2282963978", "name": "Pei-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2306768858", "name": "Shi-Lei Cao"}, {"authorId": "2261446627", "name": "Min-Zhi Li"}, {"authorId": "2064493724", "name": "Bosheng Ding"}, {"authorId": "50844234", "name": "Jue-Peng Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2383968687", "name": "Cheng-Wei Qin"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems (MAS) can solve complex problems beyond the reach of a single agent by decomposing tasks and coordinating complementary capabilities. Despite extensive research on MAS, existing work largely focuses on specific aspects or individual phases of their development and operation, resulting in fragmented perspectives with objectives and signals that are difficult to reconcile across these different settings. To bridge these disconnected perspectives, we conceptualize MAS development and operation as a three-phase lifecycle spanning design-time orchestration before execution, runtime intervention during execution, and post-hoc attribution after execution, and introduce MAS-Gate, a unified node-centric quantification applicable across these phases. Modeling each execution as a directed acyclic graph, MAS-Gate combines within-node utility, which estimates a node's contribution through changes in reference-answer likelihood, with inter-node strength, which approximates semantic uptake and potential downstream reach. We adapt this shared quantification to three objectives: providing a structure-aware dense reward for Orchestrator training, training an Editor to act on execution prefixes using paired rewards from completed edited and unedited trajectories, and supplying risk-ranked nodes as auxiliary evidence for LLM-based post-hoc attribution. Experiments spanning interaction environments, knowledge-intensive multi-hop question answering, general reasoning, and post-hoc attribution demonstrate that MAS-Gate improves workflow construction, targeted runtime editing, and LLM-assisted error localization over corresponding phase-specific baselines. Together, these results establish MAS-Gate as a unified structural interface for improving MAS generation, dynamic editing, and error attribution throughout the lifecycle."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "914f15e6585cb4169373f3b891a6159bccde065e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17740", "CorpusId": 292897127}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/914f15e6585cb4169373f3b891a6159bccde065e", "title": "Coupled Harness--Model Evolution for Scientific Equation Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17740, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2272217313", "name": "Peng-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2355335469", "name": "Jia-Quan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2352108190", "name": "En-Cheng Su"}, {"authorId": "2323315765", "name": "Yun-Bo Long"}, {"authorId": "2321477871", "name": "Zi-Hang Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2510655972", "name": "Xin-Ping Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510651534", "name": "Han Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2367176905", "name": "Jun-Chi Yu"}, {"authorId": "2362870789", "name": "Shi-Xiang Tang"}, {"authorId": "2042692068", "name": "Aoran Wang"}, {"authorId": "2371415567", "name": "Xi Chen"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used to discover scientific equations from experimental data. Such a discovery system has two parts that must work together: a library of executable computational tools and the model's tool-use policy. The tools supply the solving capability, from diagnosing structure in the data to fitting candidate equations; the policy plans and orchestrates their use. Yet existing systems adapt only one part, keeping either the toolset or the model fixed, which leaves the corresponding failures uncorrectable. We develop a coupled evolution framework that improves both parts from the same verifiable fit-error signal. In the tool-evolution phase, an agent grows an executable tool library from a small set of generic numerical operations by diagnosing its own failures: it authors a new tool when a capability is missing and revises its selection rules when the right tool went unused. In the policy-evolution phase, a small open model (roughly 3B active parameters) is fine-tuned on successful tool-use trajectories collected with the frozen library, on training formulas screened to be disjoint from all evaluation benchmarks. Ablations show the two phases are complementary: with no tool library at all the same model solves almost nothing, the tools account for most of the gain, and trajectory training adds a further gain that is largest on the most difficult problems. The full system solves 79.1/71.3\\% of LSR-Synth problems at fit tolerances $10^{-2}$/$10^{-3}$, reaches 63.8\\% pooled accuracy on the distributionally different SRSD-Feynman panel, and obtains a normalized CP3-28 aggregate of $-7.27$. These results suggest a broader recipe for scientific agents: let the model and its instruments improve each other under verifiable feedback."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4e12f90d3fa6fe05b0af7b7ebee9194254dcd0b7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19226", "CorpusId": 292903497}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4e12f90d3fa6fe05b0af7b7ebee9194254dcd0b7", "title": "Agentic Self-Awareness: Aligning Knowing, Acting, and Holding", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19226, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2157989543", "name": "Qing-Lin Chen"}, {"authorId": "50376014", "name": "Hiroaki Hayashi"}, {"authorId": "2383058705", "name": "Jia-Xin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2383150796", "name": "Chien-Sheng Wu"}], "abstract": "Frontier AI agents increasingly act in environments where seemingly solvable tasks may lack prerequisites, contain conflicting requirements, or reveal blockers only during execution. We formulate agentic self-awareness (ASA) as the alignment of task-grounded feasibility judgments (know), warranted behavior (act), and responses to reconsideration (hold), distinguishing what a task permits from what an agent can solve. We build paired feasible and blocked tasks in Terminal-Bench 2.1 and retail $\\tau^2$-bench and introduce an authorship-aware M0-M3 rubric that separates recognizing a blocker from taking the certified deferral action for the correct reason. Separate probes assess stated feasibility beliefs and responses to a single challenge. In a Terminal-Bench protocol baseline, blocker recognition reaches 84.4%, but strict self-awareness (SA) reaches only 40.6%. Harness search without model-weight updates increases strict SA from 45.3% to 93.8% on reused Terminal-Bench search tasks; selection for abstention alone, however, can reduce success on feasible tasks. Independently initialized joint-objective searches improve both blocked-task SA and feasible-task solve on their own search sets. Retail held-out gains remain limited, and Terminal-Bench blocked-task transfer is not measured. This evaluation pipeline makes the recognition-action gap measurable and sets a two-sided target for improving agent decisions under changing task evidence."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "93793a4dc01dfc67b76b2ece03d4e06648631ffd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.16523", "CorpusId": 292894645}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/93793a4dc01dfc67b76b2ece03d4e06648631ffd", "title": "EAU: Environment-Agent-User Co-evolution for Training User-Centric Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16523, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2256981123", "name": "Min Cai"}, {"authorId": "2266277013", "name": "Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2356053620", "name": "Gao Xing"}, {"authorId": "2510650143", "name": "Li-Bin Ma"}, {"authorId": "2455792863", "name": "Zhao-Hui Xu"}, {"authorId": "50183897", "name": "Xi Ye"}], "abstract": "Training LLM agents to interact with users over multiple turns is challenging because the learning signal depends not only on the agent, but also on the user simulator and environment, which jointly shape the agent's experience. Simulated users can be stochastic or inconsistent, while environments may contain unreliable tools, incomplete state coverage, and incorrect verifiers. Prior work mainly addresses the resulting instability through reward shaping or denser supervision, while treating these components as fixed. We instead introduce EAU, a meta-agent framework for environment--agent--user co-evolution. During training, EAU monitors trajectories, tool traces, rewards, and training statistics; diagnoses failures and uninformative interactions; and proposes targeted modifications to the user simulator, environment, reward design, and RL configuration. Validated changes are hot-swapped into the ongoing run, allowing the training setup to evolve together with the policy. These adaptations also persist across runs, so later training can build on previously discovered improvements rather than restarting from a fixed setup. We enable this process through an editable symbolic representation of user and environment dynamics, while the agent remains a neural LLM optimized with RL. Across three interactive-agent benchmarks and two backbone models, EAU consistently outperforms prior RL methods, improving the overall score over the strongest baseline by 14.7--15.0% and increasing successful task completion on ColBench by up to 36.7%, showing that co-evolving the training system with the policy can substantially improve multi-turn RL."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7baa4fa876ba953f9f1f9468105602cea42c48af", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17370", "CorpusId": 292896173}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7baa4fa876ba953f9f1f9468105602cea42c48af", "title": "HarnessTTT: Learn to Optimize Harness at Test-Time for Scientific Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17370, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2269426296", "name": "Ying-Zi Ma"}, {"authorId": "2146176464", "name": "Yu-Long Cao"}, {"authorId": "49088449", "name": "Nanxi Li"}, {"authorId": "2359179354", "name": "Fang Wu"}, {"authorId": "48378494", "name": "Yi-Zhe Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2347006409", "name": "James Y. Zou"}, {"authorId": "2382829310", "name": "Ye-Jin Choi"}, {"authorId": "2273519476", "name": "Chao-Wei Xiao"}], "abstract": "Scientific discovery with language models requires finding the best valid solution within a finite computational budget. We study how feedback from this search can improve the procedure used to conduct it. We introduce HarnessTTT, a test-time learning framework that trains a harness proposer while keeping the executor frozen. The proposer generates executable harnesses comprising system prompts, reusable skills, tools, middleware, and execution controls. Each harness guides a budgeted discovery rollout, whose outcome provides feedback for updating the proposer. A discovery-oriented policy-gradient objective combines gap-normalized rewards, leave-one-out advantages (RLOO), and max-sharpening to favor high-performing proposals. Generated components are validated before execution, and the search retains the best valid solution and selected harnesses across rounds. Across fourteen discovery tasks in mathematics, systems, GPU kernels, heuristic programming, astrodynamics, and biology, HarnessTTT with a frozen 9B executor improves on OpenEvolve with the same executor on all eleven benchmark tasks (e.g., from 1.17 to 2.64 on circle packing), improves on the discovery-fine-tuned Finch-9B by up to 92%, holds the best open-weight sub-10B result on nine, and reaches 0.380919 on Erd\\H{o}s minimum overlap, better than the best human reference of 0.380927. Scaling the proposer and frozen executor to 27B improves on eleven of fourteen tasks, and transferring the learned harnesses to GPT-5.5 matches or exceeds the strongest reported system of any backbone on nine of fourteen tasks. Together, these results show that harness search can itself be learned at test time: a trained proposer lets a small frozen executor search effectively, and the harnesses it finds carry over to stronger executors."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "13f98adf0521aa1c56096c60b1aa67af59345671", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.16491", "CorpusId": 292896228}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/13f98adf0521aa1c56096c60b1aa67af59345671", "title": "Drafting Agents, Not Just Tokens: Speculative Recursive Self-Improvement for Agentic Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16491, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2392974873", "name": "Xin Xu"}, {"authorId": "2411060706", "name": "Ke Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2144221741", "name": "Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2410885767", "name": "Yang-Ru Huang"}, {"authorId": "2116643796", "name": "Yang-Hao Xiao"}, {"authorId": "2162399554", "name": "Zhi-Chao Chen"}, {"authorId": "2294147935", "name": "Zhi-Sheng Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2330434826", "name": "Xun Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510733535", "name": "Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2276745414", "name": "Qian Li"}, {"authorId": "38929912", "name": "Hai-Bin Lv"}, {"authorId": "2394312357", "name": "Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2317576627", "name": "Jie Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2346896471", "name": "Zhou-Chen Lin"}], "abstract": "Improving an agent through successive revisions requires balancing exploration and evaluation under a limited budget. A narrow search can miss useful sequences of edits, while fully evaluating every intermediate version often exhausts the budget before those sequences yield a verified improvement. Inspired by the draft--verify principle of speculative decoding, we propose Speculative Recursive Self-Improvement (SpecRSI), a two-level framework coupling inner-loop agent search with outer-loop improver learning. SpecRSI drafts successors before their predecessors are fully evaluated, while adopting only fully evaluated, admissible versions. In the inner loop, Verification-Aware Tree Search (VATS) allocates the budget between further edits and outstanding checks using the predicted verified return of a continuation. In the outer loop, Group Verification Optimization (GVO) updates the improver from completed searches while keeping the real evaluator fixed. We evaluate SpecRSI on scientific optimization (including active queue management (AQM) for network infrastructure), coding, and general agent-system benchmarks. Under matched evaluation-call budgets, SpecRSI improves scientific terminal gain over Janus by 60.0%--96.3% and area under the budget curve by 32.1%--91.6%, while achieving higher means across all 50 scientific backbone--task pairs and outperforming MetaRSI-v1 by 1.30--2.70 percentage points (pp) in system score across twenty backbones. On instance-level comparisons, it further improves PyBaMM terminal gain by 142.6% and SWE-Pro success by 6.0 pp at 7.0% and 9.2% lower token cost, respectively. These results demonstrate the efficacy of the proposed approach."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ceeceeab786492ea9ccc1b9335d37129383372f2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.16078", "CorpusId": 292894201}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ceeceeab786492ea9ccc1b9335d37129383372f2", "title": "Heterogeneous Agentic Coevolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16078, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "122298411", "name": "Wenqian Ye"}, {"authorId": "11701175", "name": "Ziwei Guan"}, {"authorId": "2502615697", "name": "Zi-Jun Yang"}, {"authorId": "2459892200", "name": "Shivani Modi"}, {"authorId": "2510650681", "name": "Ci-Ci-Cong Wu"}, {"authorId": "2518823155", "name": "Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2266720726", "name": "Yu-Xin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2342644672", "name": "Zheng-Yu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2290443245", "name": "Liang Luo"}, {"authorId": "31861929", "name": "Venkateshan Ranganathan"}, {"authorId": "1423419337", "name": "Jianhui Sun"}, {"authorId": "2289996617", "name": "Yan-Tao Yao"}, {"authorId": "2290004975", "name": "Wen-Lin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2264670914", "name": "Bu-Yun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "1999317942", "name": "Ellie Wen"}, {"authorId": "1690271", "name": "Henry A. Kautz"}, {"authorId": "2265729351", "name": "Ai-Dong Zhang"}], "abstract": "Recent advances in self-improving LLM agents have demonstrated the potential of AI systems that drive their own intelligence. However, many domain-specific applications rely on specialized foundation models that possess strong domain expertise but remain dependent on human-designed improvements. Extending recursive self-improvement to these models presents a key challenge: as a specialist improves, the strategies used to advance it must also adapt. To address this challenge, we propose Heterogeneous Agentic CoEvolution (HACE), a framework that jointly evolves specialized foundation models and LLM agents through reciprocal learning. HACE improves the specialist through supervised learning while training the agent with on-policy reinforced self-training, guided by verified performance gains on the current specialist. The agent's exploration, in turn, informs subsequent specialist training, enabling both models to adapt to each other's progress. To sustain this reciprocal learning, HACE further employs evolutionary search to optimize their interaction harness, adapting how the models exchange information and select training experiences as their capabilities evolve. We evaluate HACE across three challenging domains: recommendation systems, protein function prediction in biology, and crystal stability prediction in material discovery. We show that HACE outperforms the evaluated self-improving methods on the primary task metrics across all three domains. These results shed light on heterogeneous coevolution as a universal approach to improving specialized foundation models through reciprocal interaction with a learning agent."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c19d06c396ecb9a53a01bc8a83feace3a4148921", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15915", "CorpusId": 292894504}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c19d06c396ecb9a53a01bc8a83feace3a4148921", "title": "VIEWCRAFT: Evolving What Multimodal Agents See and How They Read It", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15915, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2375625516", "name": "Bin-Xiao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2407465447", "name": "Rui-Chuan An"}, {"authorId": "2269737481", "name": "Bocheng Zou"}, {"authorId": "2214830561", "name": "Weihua Du"}, {"authorId": "2276843415", "name": "Xin-Yu Wei"}, {"authorId": "2405115995", "name": "Hang-Yu Guo"}, {"authorId": "2510650275", "name": "Yi-Fan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2147420753", "name": "Ren-Ye Yan"}, {"authorId": "2326302007", "name": "Chengqi Duan"}, {"authorId": "2331326371", "name": "Hang Hua"}], "abstract": "Reusable skills improve multimodal agents without updating model weights, but their effectiveness depends on both the evidence presented and the procedure used to read it. We introduce ViewCraft, which jointly evolves an executable evidence interface and its reading procedure. The interface selects, transforms, and organizes observations; the reader interprets the resulting views. Failure traces guide revisions, replay and paired evaluation retain useful changes, and interface projection recovers visual edits from eligible rejected joint proposals. Across eight benchmarks, ViewCraft improves over direct execution by an average of $6.1$ points in task score, with gains on every benchmark. The advantage persists at a matched input-token budget. On all five multi-source tasks, the learned interface uses 8%-15% fewer input tokens than Initial skill. The learned skills also transfer across executors and outperform direct execution in every evaluated transfer, supporting evidence construction and reading as a coupled, reusable skill."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "96ec4c8f6b5b48c3300583c61f2283163f58eb0d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15907", "CorpusId": 292895121}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/96ec4c8f6b5b48c3300583c61f2283163f58eb0d", "title": "Mind Your Own Role: Reducing Role Overstepping for Effective and Efficient Multi-Agent Collaboration via Dynamic Role Activation Steering", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15907, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2348291399", "name": "Xing-Ke Yang"}, {"authorId": "2374181744", "name": "Si-Cong Li"}, {"authorId": "2364834694", "name": "Ling-Feng Yao"}, {"authorId": "32408366", "name": "Rui Chen"}, {"authorId": "2510659125", "name": "Gen Li"}, {"authorId": "2348110940", "name": "Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2350526666", "name": "Xin Fu"}, {"authorId": "2284082603", "name": "Miao Pan"}], "abstract": "Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) have shown strong capability in solving complex tasks by assigning different agents specialized roles and corresponding subtasks. However, agents may overstep their assigned responsibilities and perform subtasks belonging to other roles, which can disrupt workflow, reduce system efficiency, and degrade task performance. To address this issue, we investigate role-related patterns in the model's internal activation space and uncover two key observations: (i) role instructions induce consistent directional shifts in activation space, and (ii) role-adherent and role-overstepping trajectories exhibit distinct activation patterns. Based on these observations, we propose Dynamic Role Activation Steering (DRAS), a lightweight and effective inference-time activation intervention approach to reduce role overstepping in multi-agent collaboration. Specifically, DRAS constructs a role steering vector and a lightweight role-overstepping detector for each role. During LLM generation, the detector estimates the probability of role overstepping from the current activation state, and DRAS dynamically adjusts the steering strength accordingly to guide the agent toward its assigned responsibilities. Experimental results show that DRAS effectively reduces the number of role-overstepping trajectories by up to 86.6% compared with Vanilla MAS, thereby improving task quality score by 10.0%--11.1%, achieving a 1.50$\\times$--1.61$\\times$ task-completion speedup, and reducing token consumption by 26.8%--38.7%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5067c9be45c28716c1b254b4906c85aaf1f964f6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15788", "CorpusId": 292895518}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5067c9be45c28716c1b254b4906c85aaf1f964f6", "title": "Agent Sociodrama: Social-Dynamics-Guided Persona Recasting for LLM Agent Teams", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15788, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2376151962", "name": "Yi-Rui Qi"}, {"authorId": "2290856471", "name": "Xiao-Ming Zhang"}, {"authorId": "13563486", "name": "Jaehong Yoon"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents are increasingly deployed as teams, yet they fail in characteristically social ways: information holders stay silent, dominant speakers anchor groups on wrong answers, and norm violations go unsanctioned. We present Agent Sociodrama, a framework for modeling and validating evolving social dynamics in LLM agent teams. Under fixed personas, agents'emotional states and directed, asymmetric relationships are updated every round by a unified external LLM judge and shape their subsequent interactions. Across six LLM backbones and five social scenarios, we assess social fidelity through classical social-theory patterns and agreement with human judgments. The resulting trajectories exhibit structured social patterns and model-specific differences. We then introduce Trajectory-Guided Persona Recasting (TPR), which diagnoses group-dynamic failures from interaction transcripts and social trajectories and recasts agent personas before rerunning the task. Across four collaborative benchmarks, TPR improves performance from both heterogeneous and randomly initialized agent persona compositions, yielding up to 83.3%p of accuracy improvement over the initial casting. On representative distributed-information cooperation and mixed-motive bargaining tasks, we outperform SOTOPIA and AgentVerse in three of four comparisons, by up to 37.5%p in Pareto rate. These results show that validated social trajectories provide actionable signals for repairing LLM agent teams."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f7f151d0c7b964c558e07fabeb5728a9270dc71c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15964", "CorpusId": 292895466}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f7f151d0c7b964c558e07fabeb5728a9270dc71c", "title": "Small but Mighty: Test Suites as Improvement Signals for Self-Evolving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15964, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328160293", "name": "Yu-Tong Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2249879747", "name": "Wei Cheng"}, {"authorId": "3007026", "name": "Wen-Chao Yu"}, {"authorId": "2313576252", "name": "Da-Wei Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2249915042", "name": "Hai-Feng Chen"}], "abstract": "A self-evolving agent rewrites its own harness and must decide which rewrites to keep, so its improvement signal is the check a rewrite has to pass. Verifying a rewrite by agent rollout is expensive and subjective, while executable checks such as the repository's own tests are too sparse to catch behavior-changing rewrites, rejecting only a quarter of them. We instead take test feedback as the executable signal, and search for test suites that are both small and strong with a quality-diversity evolutionary algorithm, so that the signal is dense and informative. TeSearch treats suite size as a behavior descriptor, so that quality is pursued at every size and a small, strong suite is reachable, and learns a transition kernel during the search that denoises the LLM as an actuator on that descriptor. Across 129 real-world modules and three backbones, TeSearch delivers the highest-quality suites of any method compared, LLM-driven evolutionary search included, and the smallest of any LLM-based one. Used as the improvement signal of a self-evolving agent, it rejects 59.9% of behavior-changing rewrites against 25.3% for the repository's tests, at a 4.0% false-alarm rate and 3.5 s per decision. The code and data are available at https://github.com/ccsnow127/TeSearch."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "524115a5b4c3cc9a81c0750a036980abfbe8fff6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15708", "CorpusId": 292894063}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/524115a5b4c3cc9a81c0750a036980abfbe8fff6", "title": "SpecForge: Recursive Self-Improvement for Coding Agents via Behavioral Specifications", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15708, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328160293", "name": "Yu-Tong Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2204622281", "name": "Hai-Feng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2304180290", "name": "Peng Gao"}, {"authorId": "2283249309", "name": "Wei Cheng"}], "abstract": "Coding agents based on frozen language models can improve their performance by refining the external context they rely on, without updating model weights. We study recursive self-improvement through behavioral specifications: natural-language code documentation that describes the behavior of program entities and guides agents in generating correct code. We formulate a self-improvement loop in which an agent generates code from its current specifications, evaluates it with execution-verified tests, and refines the specifications based on observed failures. The improved specifications then guide subsequent generation, progressively improving the agent's understanding of the codebase. However, recursive refinement is challenging because program entities are interdependent. Improving the specification of one entity can inadvertently degrade the correctness of others, a phenomenon we call output coupling. This creates a whack-a-mole effect in which local improvements cause regressions elsewhere, preventing consistent progress. We introduce SpecForge, a multi-frontier tree search framework that addresses this challenge with three mechanisms: (i) Pareto-guided state selection preserves complementary specification states and prevents premature commitment; (ii) dependency-constrained entity selection prioritizes callee-before-caller refinement; and (iii) error-diversified expansion explores multiple refinements using different subsets of test failures. On DevEval+, SpecForge achieves the best performance across five backbone models, including a 94.9% solve rate with Claude-4.5-Sonnet and a 5.7-percentage-point average improvement over the strongest baseline. The improved specifications also make the agent better at tasks outside the loop: they transfer to cross-language translation and to new-feature implementation, where they outperform the benchmark-provided requirement documents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b76555525cfc7188cbee6f2eed7803fbde6ee246", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15389", "CorpusId": 292896333}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b76555525cfc7188cbee6f2eed7803fbde6ee246", "title": "VideoEvoHarness: Self Evolving Agent Harness from Unlabeled Videos for Video Understanding", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15389, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2347179648", "name": "Wei-Xin Xu"}, {"authorId": "2492556852", "name": "Yi-Fan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2263510675", "name": "Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2300173227", "name": "Ming-Fei Rong"}, {"authorId": "2484481606", "name": "Zhen-Yu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2294147935", "name": "Zhi-Sheng Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2284669445", "name": "Sheng-Sheng Qian"}, {"authorId": "2237947496", "name": "Chang-Sheng Xu"}], "abstract": "Video understanding agents rely on a harness to acquire evidence, use tools, and reason, but improving it requires substantial effort. Recent self evolution methods seek to optimize harnesses through execution feedback to reduce human intervention. However, existing methods often rely on annotated video questions and answers or focus on updating model parameters, leaving harness evolution from unlabeled videos underexplored. VideoEvoHarness jointly evolves a Questioner and a Solver harness from unlabeled videos with frozen model parameters. The Questioner constructs questions and computes reference answers directly from video memory records. Question quality assessments and Solver execution trajectories guide question generation policy revisions. The Solver supports evolving skills, strategies, hyperparameters, and tools using execution feedback. Executable strategies define workflows in code, reducing repeated interpretation of natural language instructions. Each round, candidate harnesses are compared on identical validation questions, with validated improvements retained for later use. Experiments on benchmarks and a dedicated benchmark question set, using videos disjoint from evolution videos, show higher overall accuracy than initially and transfer gains across backbones. Overall accuracy on this set also improves progressively across rounds. This supports our foundational framework for harness evolution from unlabeled videos, opening a promising direction for self evolving video agents. Code is available at https://github.com/Wei-Xin-Xu/VideoEvoHarness."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e13e0fbcf9575bab83e4f529e5a581cd2eb05f04", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14662", "CorpusId": 292858880}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e13e0fbcf9575bab83e4f529e5a581cd2eb05f04", "title": "Does Failure Feedback Direct Agent Self-Modification?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14662, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2502175281", "name": "Rong He"}], "abstract": "Self-improving agents use failure feedback to revise their code or instructions, but gains after selection do not establish target-specific repair or generalization. We measure directedness by comparing repair of a randomly selected target failure with repair of the parent's other failures. Across 145 eligible modifications in public Darwin G\\\"odel Machine logs, we find no detectable target-specific advantage. Controlled instruction-revision experiments with small open models show that relevant feedback can improve target repair. In a second study with a pre-specified analysis plan, target feedback raised repair by 20 percentage points for Qwen3-4B relative to matched same-category feedback; the 7-point increase for Phi-4-mini was not significant. Target repair did not reliably imply held-out improvement. Held-out gains were concentrated in settings with identifiable, repairable failure mechanisms, including planted errors and natural tool-interface failures. Benefits of edit localization and regression screening varied across settings, while eight-step revision loops and GEPA ended at similar mean held-out accuracy. Evaluations should report target repair, transfer to related failures, and held-out net change separately."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7764f11267ea93764232cece7a844059352b304a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15150", "CorpusId": 292895781}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7764f11267ea93764232cece7a844059352b304a", "title": "Harmony Harness: Multi-Objective Self-Evolution of Agent Harnesses", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15150, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2258712398", "name": "Wei-Cheng Yu"}, {"authorId": "71436125", "name": "Lesly Miculicich"}, {"authorId": "40135250", "name": "Bhavana Dalvi"}, {"authorId": "2155099104", "name": "Jie-Feng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2501916210", "name": "Shuo Li"}, {"authorId": "2337690647", "name": "Matt Fredrikson"}, {"authorId": "2305619900", "name": "Tomas Pfister"}, {"authorId": "2253355959", "name": "Long T. Le"}], "abstract": "Recent work on self-evolving LLM agents treats the agent harness as an optimization target that can be iteratively improved through execution feedback. Existing approaches, however, largely optimize a single objective and evolve the harness as a monolithic artifact, limiting their ability to handle competing goals and to separate transferable design from environment-specific adaptation. We introduce Harmony Harness, a framework for multi-objective, compositional harness evolution. Harmony Harness formulates harness evolution as a multi-objective optimization problem and uses decomposition-based evolutionary search to maintain solutions across different regions of the Pareto frontier, avoiding reliance on a hand-designed structural diversity metric for repository-scale harnesses. In parallel, we introduce factorized evolution operators that separately refine general-purpose harness mechanisms and benchmark-specific adaptations, together with a dynamic composition workflow that integrates them at inference time. Across SecRepoBench and BaxBench, Harmony Harness consistently outperforms strong harness-evolution baselines, with gains exceeding 10% on both benchmarks. Our results show that explicitly modeling both objective trade-offs and harness structure yields substantially stronger and more transferable agent evolution than existing single-objective, monolithic approaches."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5a23bc0ddb5e8546f478ce57ea8e9b8200c0bd88", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15221", "CorpusId": 292896556}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5a23bc0ddb5e8546f478ce57ea8e9b8200c0bd88", "title": "HarnessNet: Feedback-driven Hierarchical Joint Harness Evolution for Multi-agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15221, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2424475953", "name": "Zhao-Yuan Qiu"}, {"authorId": "39877692", "name": "Sachith Seneviratne"}, {"authorId": "2353062280", "name": "Yu Xia"}, {"authorId": "2200576384", "name": "Saman K. Halgamuge"}], "abstract": "AI agents can evolve by using environment feedback to revise their harnesses, the software managing model inputs, tool use, memory, and execution, while keeping foundation models frozen. Existing studies focus on single agents, leaving joint harness evolution in multi-agent systems (MAS) underexplored. A single global optimizer faces growing context and workload as the system scales, while access restrictions may prevent sharing agent internals or system-wide feedback. We propose HarnessNet, a framework for hierarchical harness evolution in MAS. Its basic unit, Agentron, comprises an evolvable forward harness, a foundation model, and a local optimizer that can observe and modify only that harness. Agentrons form a layered graph through which environment feedback propagates backward, with upstream optimizers receiving node-specific feedback from the downstream layer. This decomposes joint harness evolution into smaller, coordinated tasks with separate contexts, reducing individual optimizers'context and workload while keeping access local. Optimizers need not access all agent trajectories or raw environment feedback. Experiments on AgentBench with multiple foundation models demonstrate the feasibility of training HarnessNet. Compared with a single global optimizer, HarnessNet achieves comparable performance gains while respecting local privacy and requiring fewer tokens and less time per optimizer call. A migration study shows that the evolved harnesses remain jointly effective after replacing the foundation models, suggesting transferability across models."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7f12d769c9e561fabc41a9d5297cd677405b6ff6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14495", "CorpusId": 292859426}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7f12d769c9e561fabc41a9d5297cd677405b6ff6", "title": "Reels-RSI: Program-Level Self-Improvement for Code-Rendered Explainer Videos", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14495, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2336243476", "name": "Wei Lin"}], "abstract": "Explainer videos -- a worked geometry problem, why the sky is blue, how a dynasty fell -- are usually one more target for pixel video generation. We argue they are a different kind of artifact: a program (script, storyboard and per-frame animation code) that a deterministic renderer turns into frames. A program can be read, diffed, tested and audited; pixels can only be watched. Reels-RSI is an open-source reel director for any agent harness; its self-improvement happens at the program level rather than in weights or prompts. Every revision pass is logged with a code snapshot; a retrospective pairs each check finding with the diff that fixed it and proposes lessons; a lesson that a static check can catch is compiled into a rule with a must-fail and a must-pass example, so the memory is unit-tested; and a rule learned today runs over everything made before it. A bounded, benchmark-gated step lets an agent rewrite the director's playbook, kept only when blind pairwise judgments on held-out topics prefer it; nothing enters memory without a human decision. Across 111 reels -- a field study, two playbook evolutions, a 2x2 memory ablation, a weaker director, a 48-reel learning curve and a judge-reliability study -- we find that the rule learned in the field caught four latent defects in frames authored before it existed and none after; that rules are an audit for a strong director (eight unguarded reels, no violation) and a guard for a weaker one (thirty violations unguarded versus five); that lessons shorten revision loops but do not compound or move the judge; and that the first rewrite's 11--1 verdict was a position bias in our vote design (judges pick the second-shown reel 62--81% of the time), masking scripts that traded thirteen facts for craft. We document four failure modes of the preference signal, which check caught each, and the fixes. Code, rules, lessons and data are public."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4d2d92e0743c006a7593c52b6af019960a0b10d2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33024", "CorpusId": 294227979}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4d2d92e0743c006a7593c52b6af019960a0b10d2", "title": "Evolving to Match Capabilities: Improving Harness Self-Evolution via Skill Adaptation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33024, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2484463580", "name": "Zhi-Hao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2362746418", "name": "Haojin Wang"}, {"authorId": "2315276567", "name": "Jun-Cheng Wu"}, {"authorId": "2382490514", "name": "Kuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2323857898", "name": "Yu-Kun Chen"}, {"authorId": "2240531270", "name": "Run Luo"}, {"authorId": "1831434", "name": "Yaojie Lu"}, {"authorId": "2306069928", "name": "Yu-Yin Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2356448329", "name": "Lu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2118233348", "name": "Xian-Pei Han"}], "abstract": "Harness self-evolution offers a practical route for LLM agents to improve from execution experience without updating model parameters. Its effectiveness depends on whether models can successfully act on the guidance provided by their harnesses. However, we find that harness benefits are not uniform across model capabilities: skills that improve stronger models can provide little benefit or even degrade performance for weaker ones. Across seven models and three benchmarks, we identify 103 task-skill pairs where skills improve GPT-5.6-luna by 31.1 points but degrade Gemma-4-E4B by 8.8 points. In 86% of failed weak-model runs, the violated requirement is already stated in the skill. This motivates skill adaptation: reformulating existing skills to match the executor's capabilities and make their guidance easier to execute. To achieve this, we diagnose failures in tool execution, context management, instruction following, and verification, and distill these diagnoses into a fixed meta-skill that guides adaptation during harness self-evolution. Guided by the meta-skill, a capable external rewriter, Kimi-K3, adapts skills without execution evidence, improving the three weak executors by 5.1-14.1 points over the original skills. We then incorporate this adaptation into a five-round self-evolution loop, where each model revises its own skill from execution trajectories and verifier feedback. Across seven executors, from Gemma-4-E4B to Kimi-K2.5, the meta-skill consistently improves final scores by 2.0-5.1 points over the same loop without the meta-skill. Our findings provide practical design guidance for developing more effective harness self-evolution methods."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8575a1cec19b396978efa53eed7bff1822032158", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12927", "CorpusId": 292828592}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8575a1cec19b396978efa53eed7bff1822032158", "title": "Collaborative Learning and Reasoning over Model Slices", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12927, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2379583715", "name": "Yu-Heng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2379057166", "name": "Run-De Yang"}, {"authorId": "2445701562", "name": "Zhu-Han Bao"}, {"authorId": "77005311", "name": "Qi-Ran Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2378579318", "name": "Hua-Tao Li"}, {"authorId": "2509371498", "name": "Ying Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2265075699", "name": "Lin-Na Wu"}, {"authorId": "2347555604", "name": "Jing-Ru Fan"}, {"authorId": "2283880758", "name": "Shun-Yu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2388116018", "name": "Yi-Han Li"}, {"authorId": "2377174814", "name": "Zhi Li"}, {"authorId": "2404422283", "name": "Chen Qian"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) excel across diverse tasks, benefiting from knowledge priors, in-context reasoning, and test-time computation. Despite these strengths, single-model reasoning remains vulnerable to error propagation, as later steps depend on the model's earlier outputs, leaving reasoning blind spots difficult to independently reassess. To address this limitation, we develop \\textit{collaborative learning}, encompassing the construction of heterogeneous collaborators and the learning of effective cross-model collaboration. Specifically, \\textit{model slicing} replaces a large model with smaller, heterogeneous models under a comparable parameter budget, enabling prior reasoning to be reassessed across distinct model parameterizations. \\textit{Pyramid supervision} combines protocol distillation with step-level supervision to refine reasoning and interaction decisions throughout collaboration. We evaluate our approach across four task categories: multi-hop question answering, mathematical reasoning, code generation, and open-ended constrained generation. The trained heterogeneous models achieve comparable or better task performance than the original large model and established multi-agent baselines, including reinforcement-learning-based methods, while also achieving higher inference efficiency than the multi-agent baselines and exhibiting differentiated collaboration patterns across tasks. These findings show that learned collaboration turns model heterogeneity into complementary reasoning capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6db864a0e63960d4ddc8a5f234400cbc2fd9f71f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13144", "CorpusId": 292828764}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6db864a0e63960d4ddc8a5f234400cbc2fd9f71f", "title": "Competence-Aware Orchestration for LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13144, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362382656", "name": "Song-Miao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2382826931", "name": "Yu-Cheng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2266813865", "name": "Cheng-Lin Yu"}, {"authorId": "2383948719", "name": "Hong-Xia Yang"}, {"authorId": "2362514471", "name": "Ming Li"}], "abstract": "LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) have emerged as a promising paradigm for enhancing complex reasoning through role specialization and collaboration, yet a key challenge remains: how can collaboration be adapted to a backbone's competence on the current query? This problem matters because more collaboration is not always better--richer coordination may help on difficult cases, but it also incurs additional token cost and redundant interactions. We propose a two-stage competence-aware framework for automatic MAS orchestration. It first models the LLM backbone's task-conditioned competence for the current query, and then uses this signal to adapt role activation and the communication graph. Across six benchmarks, our method achieves an average score of 93.81%. Experiments with six LLM backbones of varying competence levels further show that our method adjusts collaboration according to the inferred competence: for higher-competence backbones, it improves average accuracy from 87.22 to 89.97 while reducing token cost by 36.26%; for lower-competence backbones, it yields a further average gain of 4.58 points over existing methods. The same trend also holds on unseen backbones."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6b277d2fd5d47e365dd1ffcf7c673caf915b253a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13064", "CorpusId": 292829136}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6b277d2fd5d47e365dd1ffcf7c673caf915b253a", "title": "CB-Orchestrator: Adaptive Workflow Optimization for LLM Agents via Contextual Bandits", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13064, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2343681730", "name": "Jia-Hang Sun"}, {"authorId": "2382918145", "name": "Zhi-Wei Shang"}, {"authorId": "2507857055", "name": "Zhi-Piao Liu"}, {"authorId": "2507863263", "name": "Hong-Wei Yang"}, {"authorId": "2507864943", "name": "Guo-Qing Xie"}, {"authorId": "2383826566", "name": "Shuang Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2346815014", "name": "Zhong-Xiang Dai"}], "abstract": "Large Language Model (LLM) agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in tackling complex tasks through agentic workflows. However, manually designing these workflows is labor-intensive and lacks flexibility to adapt to diverse queries. While automated workflow optimization is a promising direction, existing methods often incur expensive API costs during training-phase feedback collection. Furthermore, they fail to adaptively reuse high-quality workflows, which creates a bottleneck for both performance and cost-effectiveness. To address these limitations, we propose CB-Orchestrator, an adaptive framework that decouples workflow generation from selection. In the first stage, we construct a diverse pool of candidate workflows via evolutionary search. In the second stage, we formulate workflow selection as a Contextual Bandit problem, which enables sample-efficient learning by balancing exploration and exploitation, thereby significantly reducing the feedback required for training. During inference, the model adaptively selects the optimal workflow tailored to each query. Evaluations across five diverse benchmarks demonstrate that CB-Orchestrator consistently outperforms all baselines. Notably, on the MATH dataset, CB-Orchestrator achieves better performance than strong recent baselines while reducing training-phase API token overhead by no less than 40.55% and total end-to-end training (including workflow pool generation) token overhead by no less than 29.73%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3cd355c9600b0232a89e6d0f5257c40db74f9299", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13361", "CorpusId": 292830518}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3cd355c9600b0232a89e6d0f5257c40db74f9299", "title": "AgentSWE: Can Coding Agents Build the Agent You Actually Want?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13361, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2445720410", "name": "Jia-Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2374412422", "name": "Hong-Jin Qian"}, {"authorId": "2398804888", "name": "Yuyang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2321653170", "name": "Jia-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2362012611", "name": "Zheng Liu"}], "abstract": "As the production of software passes into the hands of software itself, coding agents are increasingly commissioned to build agents fitted to their users'own workflows. This delegation carries a quiet assumption, that the delivered agent will faithfully serve the requirement it was built for, rather than merely appear to. No existing benchmark tests that assumption. We introduce AgentSWE, a benchmark for agent software engineering spanning three stages of the agent development lifecycle: Creation of a complete agent from a natural-language requirement, Editing of production agent codebases, and Optimization toward a measurable target. Each of its 25 tasks is framed as a commission rather than an exam: the builder iterates against a handful of illustrative cases, while acceptance exercises the frozen submission on held-out cases designed to span the full requirement, grading behavior against requirement-derived rubrics under hard pass/fail gates or, for Optimization, against the source benchmark's own objective. Evaluating a range of coding agents across models and harnesses, we find that none comes close to delivering what a requirement asks for: the gap between what a builder demonstrates and what it delivers is the norm, not the corner case. An accompanying empirical analysis of the failure trajectories identifies recurring failure modes, among them overfitting to the visible cases, hollow runtimes behind code that looks complete, and unsupported claims of completion, each a signal by which coding agents can learn to build the agents they are asked for."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cbf4bfb4cf5900d4efa54d7d77ab2619fcde149c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13870", "CorpusId": 292830834}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cbf4bfb4cf5900d4efa54d7d77ab2619fcde149c", "title": "Ask Your Advisor: Learning When to Consult Stronger Models in Agentic Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13870, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509953036", "name": "Pascal Sowietzki"}, {"authorId": "2297189635", "name": "Steffen Holter"}, {"authorId": "1414641800", "name": "Yannick Metz"}, {"authorId": "1518623384", "name": "E. Kasper"}, {"authorId": "2228208206", "name": "Raphael Baur"}, {"authorId": "2258965125", "name": "Mennatallah El-Assady"}], "abstract": "To balance task performance and computational cost, agentic workflows can use a lower-cost model for execution and selectively consult a stronger model. In such executor-advisor systems, the executor decides when to seek advice, making escalation depend on self-assessment, although current language models struggle to detect where their own reasoning fails. The need for stronger reasoning emerges during execution and varies across workflow phases. We introduce ARIA, a meta-controller that learns when to consult by balancing task success against consultation cost. Our approach formulates consultation as a sequential resource-allocation problem. At workflow contact points, ARIA maps execution evidence to a compact state abstraction and selects SILENT or CONSULT, with action values learned by Monte Carlo control from cost-penalized outcomes. Across evaluated tasks of SWE-bench Verified, Terminal-Bench 2.1, and $\\tau^2$-bench, ARIA recovers 44-81% of the executor-advisor success gap at less than half the advisor model's cost. ARIA achieves the best observed success-cost trade-off among ten other evaluated conditions, including routing and cascading methods, fixed and prompted consultation, and random placement at matched advice volume. The policies are compact and inspectable, with a single consultation-price parameter governing the success-cost trade-off without retraining either underlying model."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "42f110affe265d9a5b8fe71c8c82e343dd104ec0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14216", "CorpusId": 292859231}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/42f110affe265d9a5b8fe71c8c82e343dd104ec0", "title": "Cortex: Measurement-Gated Evolution and Pruning of Coding-Agent Skills and Rules", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14216, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510262407", "name": "Samuele Giampieri"}], "abstract": "Coding agents such as Claude Code also follow text files that developers write for them: a project file, skills the agent may choose to open, and rules that load automatically when the agent reads matching files. We call these files the agent's harness. Developers add to it whenever they correct the agent, but one success proves little: the same agent can pass a task once and fail it the next time. We present Cortex, a tool that tests changes to the harness instead of trusting them. It saves fixes from the developer's sessions as replayable tasks, finds the corrections that recur, writes one skill or rule at a time, and keeps it only if repeated runs with and without it show a net gain and no task made noticeably worse. Existing text is tested the same way before it is deleted, and Cortex neither adds nor deletes text the agent never used. We tested Cortex with Claude Haiku 4.5 on a repository built for the purpose, with conventions the model tends to break, one area with nothing to learn, and a script playing the developer. On held-out tasks, the harnesses it built made the agent follow the conventions far more often than no skills or rules did, left the comparison area unchanged, and added no text read on every turn. Rules, which load automatically, did most of the work; skills helped only when offered with the files a task touches, and those offered on every turn were rarely opened. Cortex kept none of the useless or harmful suggestions we planted, but the agent never opened the useless ones or the harmful skills, and it reached no verdict on the harmful rules, so the test shows that Cortex keeps no unused text, not that it detects damage. Under the stronger Claude Sonnet 5 a single run's harness added little, and on a real open-source project, where the agent was rarely corrected, Cortex found nothing to learn. We release the tool, the lab and its data."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1df6fa46af1591aba0910441701dcc69cdad6bfc", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14163", "CorpusId": 292859432}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1df6fa46af1591aba0910441701dcc69cdad6bfc", "title": "AutoEnvScaling: Automating the Data Flywheel with Terminal Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14163, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2355567468", "name": "Simon Yu"}, {"authorId": "2257158371", "name": "Bao-Lin Peng"}, {"authorId": "2382921553", "name": "Bo Liu"}, {"authorId": "2387715346", "name": "Zheng-Yan Shi"}, {"authorId": "2303664290", "name": "Zhao-Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2280195943", "name": "Hao Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2339987832", "name": "Qian-Hui Wu"}, {"authorId": "2363427861", "name": "Wen-Lin Yao"}, {"authorId": "3106683", "name": "Natasha Jaques"}, {"authorId": "2371299862", "name": "Wei-Yan Shi"}, {"authorId": "2256752511", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Terminal agents solve tasks in software engineering and scientific research by executing commands in a terminal. Training them via reinforcement learning (RL) requires diverse and verifiable environments, but these environments are still mostly designed by human researchers, so their supply grows only as fast as researchers can work. We introduce AutoEnvScaling, which treats environment design as a terminal task and uses agents to automate the data flywheel. A proposer agent builds new RL environments from the rollouts of the solver being trained, and the solver's new rollouts guide the next round. By turning environment generation into a terminal task, AutoEnvScaling increases the valid-environment rate by up to 25 times compared to prompt-only generation, at up to 75% lower cost per valid environment. We show that training Qwen3.5-35B-A3B on AutoEnvScaling environments from GPT-5.6-sol improves its Terminal-Bench 2.1 score by 4.3 points over the matched Tmax baseline. The broader goal of AutoEnvScaling is to enable recursive self-improvement (RSI): by making both environment generation and solving terminal tasks, it lets one model co-evolve in both roles. We show that Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, acting in both roles, gains 12.2 points on Terminal-Bench 2.1 and raises its Terminal-Bench 4.0 score from 1.6 to 3.2. The pipeline also generalizes across agentic domains, generating environments for software engineering, computer use, professional work, and GPU kernel programming. Overall, these results show a path toward agents building their own training environments, reducing human reliance and taking a core step toward RSI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6fac819b95eb96e9bf74c44e8fe102e48b084d73", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14224", "CorpusId": 292859473}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6fac819b95eb96e9bf74c44e8fe102e48b084d73", "title": "Harness^2: Recursive Agent Harnessing for an Open World", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14224, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2319451284", "name": "Rui-Yao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2258603407", "name": "Yan-Fei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2220095956", "name": "Zhongying CuiZhu"}, {"authorId": "40135250", "name": "Bhavana Dalvi"}, {"authorId": "2321601631", "name": "Yifei Ming"}, {"authorId": "2453417135", "name": "Han Yu"}, {"authorId": "2325953181", "name": "Ru-Jun Han"}, {"authorId": "2278969944", "name": "Chen-Yu Lee"}, {"authorId": "2305619900", "name": "Tomas Pfister"}], "abstract": "LLM agents'capabilities depend not only on their underlying models but also on their execution harnesses. Recursive self-improvement (RSI) offers a promising approach to automatically evolve these harnesses. However, deploying RSI in open-world settings still faces two critical challenges. First, open-world tasks are typically unseen during the harness design phase and novel at test time, leaving no ground-truth verifiers to reliably guide online harness edits. Second, the RSI process remains highly task-specific and fails to generalize to novel scenarios. We introduce Harness^2, a plug-and-play, recursive agent harnessing framework that addresses these challenges through two connected levels of recursion. Task-level recursion refines the current task's harness using a contrastive Propose-Probe-Compose step that reliably guides edits without ground-truth verifiers, and can be scaled further via parallel or sequential recursion. Harnessing-level recursion then extracts experience from these edits to update a continual improvement harness, enabling the agent to better adapt to future tasks. Across three professional benchmarks and four model-harness configurations, a single step consistently improves GLM-5 and Gemini 3.5 Flash over their base harnesses, yielding gains of up to 11.5 points on WorkBuddy-Bench, 4.5 points on JobBench, and 11.8 percentage points on LAB, with further gains as the task-level recursion budget increases."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e11bd07974cb073ca3bcb0f8906b5272d3159217", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.11299", "CorpusId": 292733507}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e11bd07974cb073ca3bcb0f8906b5272d3159217", "title": "DuplexAgent-RSI: Recursive Harness Improvement for Full-Duplex Voice Agent Collaboration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11299, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2501689746", "name": "Ying Shen"}, {"authorId": "2470701631", "name": "Yu-Xiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2329185778", "name": "Kun-Yu Feng"}, {"authorId": "2313377826", "name": "Qin-Ke Ni"}, {"authorId": "2257094753", "name": "Jia-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2260341701", "name": "Ming-Hao Hsu"}, {"authorId": "2303614129", "name": "Ju-Nan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2307482725", "name": "De-Kun Chen"}, {"authorId": "2376330262", "name": "Yu-Tong Bian"}, {"authorId": "2758425", "name": "Zhi-Zheng Wu"}], "abstract": "Voice agents are converging on a collaboration pattern: a full-duplex interaction model stays on the live channel as the entry to the conversation, while search, reasoning, and coding are handled through asynchronous delegation. A duplex model supports continuous listening and speaking, but complex reasoning and tool use may exceed its capabilities. A coding agent can plan and execute extended tasks, but its sequential interface is a poor fit for live conversation. Combining them requires a harness that coordinates task acceptance, progress, cancellation, replacement, and result delivery while keeping the conversation responsive. Existing harnesses often rely on coupled heuristics, making them difficult to improve systematically from evidence. We present DuplexAgent, a full-duplex collaboration system whose harness expresses this workflow as six editable modules, and Duplex-Harness-RSI, a closed loop that revises them from interaction traces. A simulator automatically generates timed test conversations, runs the system, and produces failure traces that identify the collaboration modules requiring repair. Reasoning LLMs and coding agents in the delegation pool also serve the improvement loop: the Exam Planner selects the next tests from observed weaknesses and the repair archive, and the Harness Editor proposes targeted module changes. The capabilities that serve the user thus also improve the system's coordination. Experiments on intelligence, agentic, and duplex benchmarks show that DuplexAgent combines continuous interaction with difficult reasoning and complex task execution, achieving stronger spoken-knowledge and executable-tool scores than the compared delegated systems while maintaining strong interruption response. A harness ablation further shows that this modular, verifiable loop outperforms the initial harness and repeated editing that lacks its diagnosis and repair archive."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "781222883a8306b608949301ba4ea625cd94614b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.11655", "CorpusId": 292737930}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/781222883a8306b608949301ba4ea625cd94614b", "title": "Harness Evolution Hits a Ceiling: When Weight Training Should Begin", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11655, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2283776098", "name": "Yuan Tian"}, {"authorId": "2385446371", "name": "Bing Hu"}, {"authorId": "2353314544", "name": "Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2195352476", "name": "Bing-Hang Lu"}, {"authorId": "2408941235", "name": "Fang Wu"}], "abstract": "Improving a long-horizon LLM agent means evolving the harness around a frozen model or training its weights. We let a self-evolving harness make the system stronger first, then cross seed and evolved harnesses with base and trained weights to learn which gains the trained model keeps and which still need the runtime. We show that the right lever can be read off the agent's failure composition: labelling failed trajectories by the first signal that fires separates process failures (blocked calls, loops, exhausted step budgets) from content failures (a delivered plan that is poor). Harness evolution repairs the former, the behaviour it instils can be trained into the weights, and content failures are what weight training is for. On DeepPlanning, a self-evolving harness loop lifts the held-out score of Qwen3.5-4B from 0.16 to 0.30 and of Qwen3.5-9B from 0.32 to 0.44; for 4B, held-out delivery rises from 55% to 90% while content failures are left for the weights. LoRA adapters trained on evolved-harness trajectories internalise the gain: under the original harness they add +0.13 on held-out tasks for both sizes; on 4B they stack with the harness to more than double the held-out score, and on 9B the adapter alone matches the full evolution line, cutting content failures from a quarter of trajectories to one in twenty. A placebo adapter trained on answer-shuffled trajectories falls below the base model. The loop transfers to WebArena-Lite (+0.09 on 117 unseen tasks), where the gain lives in what the model sees and adapters do not add to it. The result is a diagnose-then-intervene rule applied twice: read the failure composition to choose between harness and weights, then read what the accepted edits changed to decide which gains to train in. Scores are four-rollout means against fresh anchors, same-night except where marked, across eight models from six families and two benchmarks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6116dcf16c15992d5439b93a4fbec5db1224ecf1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12176", "CorpusId": 292738246}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6116dcf16c15992d5439b93a4fbec5db1224ecf1", "title": "Recursive Self-Improvement through Multi-Agent Self-Supervision", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12176, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2110007977", "name": "Hyunin Lee"}, {"authorId": "2223017366", "name": "Jinglue Xu"}, {"authorId": "2360171459", "name": "Jeffrey Seely"}, {"authorId": "2263095684", "name": "Donghyun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2363570697", "name": "Somayeh Sojoudi"}, {"authorId": "2257206424", "name": "Matei A. Zaharia"}, {"authorId": "2326422916", "name": "Yu-Jin Tang"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) of a model on non-verifiable tasks, such as open-ended research, faces a supervision bottleneck when its outputs exceed what even human experts can reliably assess, leaving the model itself (optimizee) as the best available optimizer and evaluator. However, a single model instance struggles to critique and improve its own complex reasoning under this homogeneous loop. To address this, we propose Multi-Agent Self-Supervision (MASS), an RSI method that alternates between evolutionary workflow optimization and supervised fine-tuning on self-generated trajectories. Guided by early findings that multi-agent topologies excel at complex reasoning, MASS prompts a single base model to iteratively propose, execute, and self-evaluate multi-agent workflows. Through an evolutionary search constrained by structural guardrails, the model optimizes these computational-graph-like orchestrations, discovering the most effective distinct roles and information routing for a given task. Over two MASS cycles with Qwen3.6-27B, the model achieves 1.2-1.6x higher performance per output tokens on four open-ended public benchmarks. Because the improved model subsequently acts as a better optimizer and evaluator, this alternating framework enables a continuous, recursive bootstrapping of the model's capabilities. Moreover, multi-agent traces are also more training-efficient: a student trained on them outperforms a single-agent student trained on 1.4x more training tokens. These findings suggest that jointly learning orchestration and bounded subagent execution from multi-agent trajectories can provide an effective signal for RSI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "192fd3b5d9dbad85b386fdda9c244e0ad67f3e3d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12341", "CorpusId": 292737748}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/192fd3b5d9dbad85b386fdda9c244e0ad67f3e3d", "title": "Can AI Agents Learn Their Way to the Top? Evaluating Heuristic Learning in a Long-Running Game Agent Competition", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12341, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2383097217", "name": "Kai-Sen Yang"}, {"authorId": "2445721255", "name": "Qing-Le Liu"}, {"authorId": "2502195351", "name": "Ke-Jin Wang"}, {"authorId": "2375397262", "name": "Yi-Chen Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2509331205", "name": "Jie-Ming Li"}, {"authorId": "2412815574", "name": "Sheng-Han Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2509362532", "name": "Rui-Ze Yang"}, {"authorId": "2494136801", "name": "Bo Yang"}, {"authorId": "2509317357", "name": "Heng Gong"}, {"authorId": "2506648206", "name": "Xiang Gao"}, {"authorId": "2509365018", "name": "Lan-Yue Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2371114650", "name": "Kai-Yu Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2504006281", "name": "Zhuo-Chen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2509358622", "name": "Shao-Xuan Li"}, {"authorId": "2509349517", "name": "Cheng-Xi Li"}, {"authorId": "2320707357", "name": "Yong Yan"}, {"authorId": "2509366300", "name": "Wei-Xuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2313639472", "name": "Tian-Yuan Luo"}, {"authorId": "2499252664", "name": "Si-Meng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2537322", "name": "You-Jie Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2404231718", "name": "Si-Han Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2455430554", "name": "Sheng-Yuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2371219369", "name": "Huan-Ang Gao"}, {"authorId": "2214082934", "name": "Jiazheng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2509345208", "name": "Xiao-Hui Xie"}, {"authorId": "2509387122", "name": "Wen-Tao Han"}, {"authorId": "2253869803", "name": "Hong-Ning Wang"}], "abstract": "Adversarial games have driven advances from heuristic search to reinforcement learning, yet learning and adapting strategies from limited samples remain challenging. AI agents offer an alternative by turning game experience into revisions of executable policies. Building on heuristic learning (HL), we formalize Adversarial Heuristic Learning (AHL), a paradigm that uses AI agents as learning engines to refine game policies and supporting software while keeping model weights fixed. We introduce AAArena, a benchmark comprising 12 authentic adversarial games and 1,920 archived human programs, with an evaluation protocol modeled on real-world game competitions. Agents interpret rules, choose opponents, analyze replays, and revise game agents to achieve their highest ranking within fixed match and evaluation budgets. We evaluate \\val{completedmodels} model and harness configurations: Opus5.5 with Claude Code earns 6 gold medals, while no evaluated configuration tops the remaining 6 human ladders. Performance is generally weaker in games with more complex rule specifications. Further experiments show that opponent selection and dense feedback support policy improvement, and that agents learn from both on-policy replays of their own matches and off-policy replays of other players'matches. These results highlight HL's potential in adversarial games and identify persistent challenges in game understanding, strategy implementation, and long-horizon policy development."}}]}
